 Entity Solid arrows point from a procedure to one which it calls. Dashed \narrows point from an interface to procedures which implement that interface.\nThis could include the module procedures in a generic interface or the\nimplementation in a submodule of an interface in a parent module. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Source Code subroutine refresh_host ( h ) !! Lightweight host refresh that keeps GPU data mapped. type ( solver_handle_t ), pointer , intent ( inout ) :: h if (. not . h % is_initialised ) return if ( h % host_is_current ) return if ( h % on_device ) then call solver_update_host ( h % state ) h % host_is_current = . true . end if end subroutine refresh_host","tags":"","url":"proc/refresh_host~2.html"},{"title":"rki_destroy – Rakali","text":"private  subroutine rki_destroy(c_handle) bind(c, name=\"rki_destroy\") Destroy the simulation and free the handle. Nulls c_handle.\nIdempotent on null/already-destroyed handles.\nStill call destroy to handle the null case gracefully.\nMust do full exit_data before deallocate to unmap GPU memory and\navoid “partially present” errors on a future create. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type(c_ptr), intent(inout) :: c_handle Calls proc~~rki_destroy~~CallsGraph proc~rki_destroy rki_destroy get_knowledge get_knowledge proc~rki_destroy->get_knowledge proc~handle_check handle_check proc~rki_destroy->proc~handle_check proc~handle_destroy handle_destroy proc~rki_destroy->proc~handle_destroy proc~solver_exit_data solver_exit_data proc~rki_destroy->proc~solver_exit_data proc~state_destroy state_t%state_destroy proc~rki_destroy->proc~state_destroy proc~ml_dynamics_workspaces_cleanup ml_dynamics_workspaces_cleanup proc~solver_exit_data->proc~ml_dynamics_workspaces_cleanup proc~ml_horizontal_viscosity_cleanup ml_horizontal_viscosity_cleanup proc~solver_exit_data->proc~ml_horizontal_viscosity_cleanup proc~solver_tracers_exit_data solver_tracers_exit_data proc~solver_exit_data->proc~solver_tracers_exit_data proc~tracer_scratch_destroy tracer_scratch_destroy proc~solver_exit_data->proc~tracer_scratch_destroy proc~barotropic_state_destroy barotropic_state_t%barotropic_state_destroy proc~state_destroy->proc~barotropic_state_destroy proc~remap_workspace_cleanup remap_workspace_cleanup proc~ml_dynamics_workspaces_cleanup->proc~remap_workspace_cleanup proc~scratch_array_destroy scratch_array_destroy proc~tracer_scratch_destroy->proc~scratch_array_destroy Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Interface Interface Type Bound Procedure Type Bound Procedure Unknown Procedure Type Unknown Procedure Type Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a procedure to one which it calls. Dashed \narrows point from an interface to procedures which implement that interface.\nThis could include the module procedures in a generic interface or the\nimplementation in a submodule of an interface in a parent module. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Variables Type Visibility Attributes Name Initial type( solver_handle_t ), private, pointer :: h integer, private :: status Source Code subroutine rki_destroy ( c_handle ) bind ( c , name = \"rki_destroy\" ) !! Destroy the simulation and free the handle. Nulls c_handle. !! Idempotent on null/already-destroyed handles. type ( c_ptr ), intent ( inout ) :: c_handle type ( solver_handle_t ), pointer :: h integer :: status status = handle_check ( c_handle , h ) if ( status /= HANDLE_OK ) then !! Still call destroy to handle the null case gracefully. call handle_destroy ( c_handle ) return end if !! Must do full exit_data before deallocate to unmap GPU memory and !! avoid \"partially present\" errors on a future create. if ( h % on_device ) then call solver_exit_data ( h % state ) h % on_device = . false . end if if ( h % is_initialised ) call h % state % destroy () call get_knowledge () call handle_destroy ( c_handle ) end subroutine rki_destroy","tags":"","url":"proc/rki_destroy.html"},{"title":"rki_get_grid_info – Rakali","text":"private  subroutine rki_get_grid_info(c_handle, nx, ny, nghost, dx, dy) bind(c, name=\"rki_get_grid_info\") Return physical grid dimensions (excluding ghost cells). Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type(c_ptr), intent(in), value :: c_handle integer(kind=c_int), intent(out) :: nx integer(kind=c_int), intent(out) :: ny integer(kind=c_int), intent(out) :: nghost real(kind=c_double), intent(out) :: dx real(kind=c_double), intent(out) :: dy Calls proc~~rki_get_grid_info~~CallsGraph proc~rki_get_grid_info rki_get_grid_info proc~handle_check handle_check proc~rki_get_grid_info->proc~handle_check Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Interface Interface Type Bound Procedure Type Bound Procedure Unknown Procedure Type Unknown Procedure Type Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a procedure to one which it calls. Dashed \narrows point from an interface to procedures which implement that interface.\nThis could include the module procedures in a generic interface or the\nimplementation in a submodule of an interface in a parent module. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Variables Type Visibility Attributes Name Initial type( solver_handle_t ), private, pointer :: h integer, private :: status Source Code subroutine rki_get_grid_info ( c_handle , nx , ny , nghost , dx , dy ) & bind ( c , name = \"rki_get_grid_info\" ) !! Return physical grid dimensions (excluding ghost cells). type ( c_ptr ), intent ( in ), value :: c_handle integer ( c_int ), intent ( out ) :: nx , ny , nghost real ( c_double ), intent ( out ) :: dx , dy type ( solver_handle_t ), pointer :: h integer :: status status = handle_check ( c_handle , h ) if ( status == HANDLE_OK . and . h % is_initialised ) then nx = h % state % grid % nx_phys ny = h % state % grid % ny_phys nghost = h % state % grid % nghost dx = h % state % grid % dx dy = h % state % grid % dy else nx = 0 ny = 0 nghost = 0 dx = 0.0_wp dy = 0.0_wp end if end subroutine rki_get_grid_info","tags":"","url":"proc/rki_get_grid_info.html"},{"title":"rki_sync_from_device – Rakali","text":"private  subroutine rki_sync_from_device(c_handle) bind(c, name=\"rki_sync_from_device\") Full device-to-host sync that removes data from GPU. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type(c_ptr), intent(in), value :: c_handle Calls proc~~rki_sync_from_device~~CallsGraph proc~rki_sync_from_device rki_sync_from_device proc~handle_check handle_check proc~rki_sync_from_device->proc~handle_check proc~solver_exit_data solver_exit_data proc~rki_sync_from_device->proc~solver_exit_data proc~ml_dynamics_workspaces_cleanup ml_dynamics_workspaces_cleanup proc~solver_exit_data->proc~ml_dynamics_workspaces_cleanup proc~ml_horizontal_viscosity_cleanup ml_horizontal_viscosity_cleanup proc~solver_exit_data->proc~ml_horizontal_viscosity_cleanup proc~solver_tracers_exit_data solver_tracers_exit_data proc~solver_exit_data->proc~solver_tracers_exit_data proc~tracer_scratch_destroy tracer_scratch_destroy proc~solver_exit_data->proc~tracer_scratch_destroy proc~remap_workspace_cleanup remap_workspace_cleanup proc~ml_dynamics_workspaces_cleanup->proc~remap_workspace_cleanup proc~scratch_array_destroy scratch_array_destroy proc~tracer_scratch_destroy->proc~scratch_array_destroy Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Interface Interface Type Bound Procedure Type Bound Procedure Unknown Procedure Type Unknown Procedure Type Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a procedure to one which it calls. Dashed \narrows point from an interface to procedures which implement that interface.\nThis could include the module procedures in a generic interface or the\nimplementation in a submodule of an interface in a parent module. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Variables Type Visibility Attributes Name Initial type( solver_handle_t ), private, pointer :: h integer, private :: status Source Code subroutine rki_sync_from_device ( c_handle ) bind ( c , name = \"rki_sync_from_device\" ) !! Full device-to-host sync that removes data from GPU. type ( c_ptr ), intent ( in ), value :: c_handle type ( solver_handle_t ), pointer :: h integer :: status status = handle_check ( c_handle , h ) if ( status /= HANDLE_OK ) return if ( h % is_initialised . and . h % on_device ) then call solver_exit_data ( h % state ) h % on_device = . false . h % host_is_current = . true . end if end subroutine rki_sync_from_device","tags":"","url":"proc/rki_sync_from_device.html"},{"title":"rki_sync_host – Rakali","text":"private  subroutine rki_sync_host(c_handle) bind(c, name=\"rki_sync_host\") Lightweight device-to-host sync for callbacks. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type(c_ptr), intent(in), value :: c_handle Calls proc~~rki_sync_host~~CallsGraph proc~rki_sync_host rki_sync_host proc~handle_check handle_check proc~rki_sync_host->proc~handle_check proc~solver_update_host solver_update_host proc~rki_sync_host->proc~solver_update_host Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Interface Interface Type Bound Procedure Type Bound Procedure Unknown Procedure Type Unknown Procedure Type Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a procedure to one which it calls. Dashed \narrows point from an interface to procedures which implement that interface.\nThis could include the module procedures in a generic interface or the\nimplementation in a submodule of an interface in a parent module. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Variables Type Visibility Attributes Name Initial type( solver_handle_t ), private, pointer :: h integer, private :: status Source Code subroutine rki_sync_host ( c_handle ) bind ( c , name = \"rki_sync_host\" ) !! Lightweight device-to-host sync for callbacks. type ( c_ptr ), intent ( in ), value :: c_handle type ( solver_handle_t ), pointer :: h integer :: status status = handle_check ( c_handle , h ) if ( status /= HANDLE_OK ) return if ( h % is_initialised . and . h % on_device . and . . not . h % host_is_current ) then call solver_update_host ( h % state ) h % host_is_current = . true . end if end subroutine rki_sync_host","tags":"","url":"proc/rki_sync_host.html"},{"title":"accumulate_boundary_mass_out – Rakali","text":"public  subroutine accumulate_boundary_mass_out(state, stage_weight) Accumulate the net volume that left the domain this RK stage into state%barotropic%mass_out .  By the divergence theorem the boundary\noutflux equals -Σ_interior flux_h·area (interior face fluxes\ntelescope), and flux_h is the SAME continuity tendency the update\nconsumes ( h += dt·flux_h ), so with the matching RK stage weight\nthis closes the mass budget to round-off — the console Error then\nmeasures only genuine non-conservation (e.g. dry-cell clamps). Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( state_t ), intent(inout) :: state real(kind=wp), intent(in) :: stage_weight Stage contribution to the step’s h update (0.5 for each SSP-RK2\nstage: h&#94;{n+1} = h&#94;n + 0.5·dt·(flux_h&#94;1 + flux_h&#94;2)). Calls proc~~accumulate_boundary_mass_out~~CallsGraph proc~accumulate_boundary_mass_out accumulate_boundary_mass_out reduce reduce proc~accumulate_boundary_mass_out->reduce Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Interface Interface Type Bound Procedure Type Bound Procedure Unknown Procedure Type Unknown Procedure Type Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a procedure to one which it calls. Dashed \narrows point from an interface to procedures which implement that interface.\nThis could include the module procedures in a generic interface or the\nimplementation in a submodule of an interface in a parent module. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Called by proc~~accumulate_boundary_mass_out~~CalledByGraph proc~accumulate_boundary_mass_out accumulate_boundary_mass_out proc~solver_multilayer_rk_stage solver_multilayer_rk_stage proc~solver_multilayer_rk_stage->proc~accumulate_boundary_mass_out proc~solver_step_impl solver_step_impl proc~solver_step_impl->proc~accumulate_boundary_mass_out proc~solver_step solver_step proc~solver_step->proc~solver_step_impl proc~solver_step_multilayer_impl solver_step_multilayer_impl proc~solver_step->proc~solver_step_multilayer_impl proc~solver_step_multilayer_impl->proc~solver_multilayer_rk_stage proc~driver_run_coastal driver_run_coastal proc~driver_run_coastal->proc~solver_step proc~rki_advance rki_advance proc~rki_advance->proc~solver_step proc~rki_step rki_step proc~rki_step->proc~solver_step proc~driver_run driver_run proc~driver_run->proc~driver_run_coastal Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Interface Interface Type Bound Procedure Type Bound Procedure Unknown Procedure Type Unknown Procedure Type Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a procedure to one which it calls. Dashed \narrows point from an interface to procedures which implement that interface.\nThis could include the module procedures in a generic interface or the\nimplementation in a submodule of an interface in a parent module. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Variables Type Visibility Attributes Name Initial real(kind=wp), private :: acc real(kind=wp), private :: area integer, private :: i integer, private :: i_hi integer, private :: i_lo integer, private :: j integer, private :: j_hi integer, private :: j_lo integer, private :: ng Source Code subroutine accumulate_boundary_mass_out ( state , stage_weight ) !! Accumulate the net volume that left the domain this RK stage into !! `state%barotropic%mass_out`.  By the divergence theorem the boundary !! outflux equals `-Σ_interior flux_h·area` (interior face fluxes !! telescope), and `flux_h` is the SAME continuity tendency the update !! consumes (`h += dt·flux_h`), so with the matching RK stage weight !! this closes the mass budget to round-off — the console `Error` then !! measures only genuine non-conservation (e.g. dry-cell clamps). type ( state_t ), intent ( inout ) :: state real ( wp ), intent ( in ) :: stage_weight !! Stage contribution to the step's h update (0.5 for each SSP-RK2 !! stage: h&#94;{n+1} = h&#94;n + 0.5·dt·(flux_h&#94;1 + flux_h&#94;2)). integer :: i , j , ng , i_lo , i_hi , j_lo , j_hi real ( wp ) :: acc , area ng = state % grid % nghost i_lo = ng + 1 i_hi = state % grid % nx_total - ng j_lo = ng + 1 j_hi = state % grid % ny_total - ng area = state % grid % dx * state % grid % dy acc = 0.0_wp do concurrent ( j = j_lo : j_hi , i = i_lo : i_hi ) reduce ( + : acc ) acc = acc + state % barotropic % flux_h ( i , j ) end do ! flux_h is d h/dt; Σ·area is the advective d(mass)/dt.  Outflux ! (positive = leaving) is its negative, times the stage's dt weight. state % barotropic % mass_out = state % barotropic % mass_out & - stage_weight * state % dt * acc * area state % barotropic % mass_out_tracked = . true . end subroutine accumulate_boundary_mass_out","tags":"","url":"proc/accumulate_boundary_mass_out.html"},{"title":"accumulate_mass_src – Rakali","text":"public  subroutine accumulate_mass_src(state, stage_weight, evap_mass_sink) Accumulate the net volume ADDED to the domain this RK stage by\nsurface sources into state%barotropic%mass_src . Sources tracked:\n  - Precipitation: h += dt·P → contributes +stage_weight·dt·Σ P·area (positive when P > 0, i.e. rain adds mass).\n  - Evaporative mass sink (when evap_mass_sink=.true. ): the kernel\n    removes dt·max(E,0) metres from the column, so this contributes −stage_weight·dt·Σ max(E,0)·area (negative; reduces mass_src).\n    When evap_mass_sink=.false. (default virtual-salt-flux mode) the\n    evaporation does NOT change h , so there is nothing to track here. Sign convention (mirrors accumulate_boundary_mass_out ):\n  mass_src > 0  means sources have added net volume to the domain. Forcing off ( state%forcing%active = .false. ) → no precipitation,\nno evaporation → accumulator is a no-op and mass_src stays 0,\nwhich is correct (the budget still closes to round-off). Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( state_t ), intent(inout) :: state real(kind=wp), intent(in) :: stage_weight Stage contribution (0.5 per SSP-RK2 stage). logical, intent(in) :: evap_mass_sink Whether the evaporative mass-sink mode is active (real water\nremoval from h ); when .false. the virtual-salt-flux mode\nleaves h unchanged, so evap contributes nothing here. Calls proc~~accumulate_mass_src~~CallsGraph proc~accumulate_mass_src accumulate_mass_src reduce reduce proc~accumulate_mass_src->reduce Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Interface Interface Type Bound Procedure Type Bound Procedure Unknown Procedure Type Unknown Procedure Type Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a procedure to one which it calls. Dashed \narrows point from an interface to procedures which implement that interface.\nThis could include the module procedures in a generic interface or the\nimplementation in a submodule of an interface in a parent module. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Called by proc~~accumulate_mass_src~~CalledByGraph proc~accumulate_mass_src accumulate_mass_src proc~solver_multilayer_rk_stage solver_multilayer_rk_stage proc~solver_multilayer_rk_stage->proc~accumulate_mass_src proc~solver_step_impl solver_step_impl proc~solver_step_impl->proc~accumulate_mass_src proc~solver_step solver_step proc~solver_step->proc~solver_step_impl proc~solver_step_multilayer_impl solver_step_multilayer_impl proc~solver_step->proc~solver_step_multilayer_impl proc~solver_step_multilayer_impl->proc~solver_multilayer_rk_stage proc~driver_run_coastal driver_run_coastal proc~driver_run_coastal->proc~solver_step proc~rki_advance rki_advance proc~rki_advance->proc~solver_step proc~rki_step rki_step proc~rki_step->proc~solver_step proc~driver_run driver_run proc~driver_run->proc~driver_run_coastal Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Interface Interface Type Bound Procedure Type Bound Procedure Unknown Procedure Type Unknown Procedure Type Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a procedure to one which it calls. Dashed \narrows point from an interface to procedures which implement that interface.\nThis could include the module procedures in a generic interface or the\nimplementation in a submodule of an interface in a parent module. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Variables Type Visibility Attributes Name Initial real(kind=wp), private :: acc real(kind=wp), private :: area integer, private :: i integer, private :: i_hi integer, private :: i_lo integer, private :: j integer, private :: j_hi integer, private :: j_lo integer, private :: ng Source Code subroutine accumulate_mass_src ( state , stage_weight , evap_mass_sink ) !! Accumulate the net volume ADDED to the domain this RK stage by !! surface sources into `state%barotropic%mass_src`. !! !! Sources tracked: !!   - Precipitation: `h += dt·P` → contributes `+stage_weight·dt·Σ P·area` !!     (positive when P > 0, i.e. rain adds mass). !!   - Evaporative mass sink (when `evap_mass_sink=.true.`): the kernel !!     removes `dt·max(E,0)` metres from the column, so this contributes !!     `−stage_weight·dt·Σ max(E,0)·area` (negative; reduces mass_src). !!     When `evap_mass_sink=.false.` (default virtual-salt-flux mode) the !!     evaporation does NOT change `h`, so there is nothing to track here. !! !! Sign convention (mirrors `accumulate_boundary_mass_out`): !!   mass_src > 0  means sources have added net volume to the domain. !! !! Forcing off (`state%forcing%active = .false.`) → no precipitation, !! no evaporation → accumulator is a no-op and `mass_src` stays 0, !! which is correct (the budget still closes to round-off). type ( state_t ), intent ( inout ) :: state real ( wp ), intent ( in ) :: stage_weight !! Stage contribution (0.5 per SSP-RK2 stage). logical , intent ( in ) :: evap_mass_sink !! Whether the evaporative mass-sink mode is active (real water !! removal from `h`); when `.false.` the virtual-salt-flux mode !! leaves `h` unchanged, so evap contributes nothing here. integer :: i , j , ng , i_lo , i_hi , j_lo , j_hi real ( wp ) :: acc , area if (. not . state % forcing % active ) then state % barotropic % mass_src_tracked = . true . return end if ng = state % grid % nghost i_lo = ng + 1 i_hi = state % grid % nx_total - ng j_lo = ng + 1 j_hi = state % grid % ny_total - ng area = state % grid % dx * state % grid % dy acc = 0.0_wp if ( evap_mass_sink ) then ! Net freshwater flux = precip − evap (evap_minus_precip stores E−P). ! Precip  (E−P < 0): adds mass → src += dt·|P|·area (P = -(E-P)) ! Evap    (E−P > 0): removes mass → src −= dt·E·area ! Combined: net h increment from freshwater = dt·(−evap_minus_precip) ! BUT: the kernel only acts on E (removes min(dt·E, avail)); for the ! budget we track the INTENDED flux = dt·(P − E) = dt·(−(E−P)) per cell. ! Precipitation is always added in full (no availability limit) while ! evaporation is clamped; we track the intended field here (same as the ! conservative-rounding in the actual kernel for the precip leg). ! → acc = Σ_interior (−evap_minus_precip(i,j)) = Σ (P − E) per cell. do concurrent ( j = j_lo : j_hi , i = i_lo : i_hi ) reduce ( + : acc ) acc = acc - state % forcing % evap_minus_precip ( i , j ) end do else ! Virtual-salt-flux mode: only precipitation changes h. do concurrent ( j = j_lo : j_hi , i = i_lo : i_hi ) reduce ( + : acc ) acc = acc + state % forcing % precipitation ( i , j ) end do end if state % barotropic % mass_src = state % barotropic % mass_src & + stage_weight * state % dt * acc * area state % barotropic % mass_src_tracked = . true . end subroutine accumulate_mass_src","tags":"","url":"proc/accumulate_mass_src.html"},{"title":"solver_enter_data – Rakali","text":"public  subroutine solver_enter_data(state) Copy state arrays to GPU device. Called once at simulation start;\nall subsequent computation runs on device until solver_exit_data. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( state_t ), intent(inout) :: state Solution state to place on device Calls proc~~solver_enter_data~~CallsGraph proc~solver_enter_data solver_enter_data proc~solver_tracers_enter_data solver_tracers_enter_data proc~solver_enter_data->proc~solver_tracers_enter_data proc~tracer_scratch_ensure tracer_scratch_ensure proc~solver_enter_data->proc~tracer_scratch_ensure proc~scratch_array_ensure scratch_array_ensure proc~tracer_scratch_ensure->proc~scratch_array_ensure Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Interface Interface Type Bound Procedure Type Bound Procedure Unknown Procedure Type Unknown Procedure Type Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a procedure to one which it calls. Dashed \narrows point from an interface to procedures which implement that interface.\nThis could include the module procedures in a generic interface or the\nimplementation in a submodule of an interface in a parent module. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Called by proc~~solver_enter_data~~CalledByGraph proc~solver_enter_data solver_enter_data proc~driver_run_coastal driver_run_coastal proc~driver_run_coastal->proc~solver_enter_data proc~flush_restart flush_restart proc~driver_run_coastal->proc~flush_restart proc~flush_snapshot flush_snapshot proc~driver_run_coastal->proc~flush_snapshot proc~ensure_device ensure_device proc~ensure_device->proc~solver_enter_data proc~flush_restart->proc~solver_enter_data proc~flush_snapshot->proc~solver_enter_data proc~rki_finalize rki_finalize proc~rki_finalize->proc~solver_enter_data proc~driver_run driver_run proc~driver_run->proc~driver_run_coastal proc~rki_advance rki_advance proc~rki_advance->proc~ensure_device proc~rki_create_from_namelist rki_create_from_namelist proc~rki_create_from_namelist->proc~rki_finalize proc~rki_create_from_namelist_string rki_create_from_namelist_string proc~rki_create_from_namelist_string->proc~rki_finalize proc~rki_step rki_step proc~rki_step->proc~ensure_device Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Interface Interface Type Bound Procedure Type Bound Procedure Unknown Procedure Type Unknown Procedure Type Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a procedure to one which it calls. Dashed \narrows point from an interface to procedures which implement that interface.\nThis could include the module procedures in a generic interface or the\nimplementation in a submodule of an interface in a parent module. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Variables Type Visibility Attributes Name Initial integer, private :: it logical, private :: want_hdiff logical, private :: want_ppm logical, private :: want_upwind logical, private :: want_weno Source Code subroutine solver_enter_data ( state ) !! Copy state arrays to GPU device. Called once at simulation start; !! all subsequent computation runs on device until solver_exit_data. type ( state_t ), intent ( inout ) :: state !! Solution state to place on device integer :: it logical :: want_ppm , want_upwind , want_hdiff , want_weno ! Map the parent state struct first so the device knows the shape of ! state_t; otherwise attaching a sub-struct creates a \"partial ! presence\" condition that errors out when kernels reference state ! from an `!$acc parallel loop`. !$acc enter data copyin(state) !$acc enter data copyin(state%barotropic%h, state%barotropic%hu, state%barotropic%hv, state%barotropic%b, & !$acc&                  state%barotropic%flux_h, state%barotropic%flux_hu, state%barotropic%flux_hv, & !$acc&                  state%barotropic%mass_flux_x, state%barotropic%mass_flux_y, & !$acc&                  state%barotropic%h0, state%barotropic%hu0, state%barotropic%hv0) ! Surface forcing: value-type member, so only its allocatable arrays ! need explicit attaches (scalars/descriptors came with copyin(state)). !$acc enter data copyin(state%forcing%wind_x, state%forcing%wind_y, & !$acc&                  state%forcing%atm_pressure, & !$acc&                  state%forcing%precipitation, & !$acc&                  state%forcing%heat_flux, & !$acc&                  state%forcing%evap_minus_precip) ! Chapman BC eta_old (one slot per cell along each edge). Always ! mapped because bc_init_chapman unconditionally allocates all four ! arrays; the kernel only reads them when the matching edge is ! BC_CHAPMAN. !$acc enter data copyin(state%bc%eta_old_west, state%bc%eta_old_east, & !$acc&                  state%bc%eta_old_south, state%bc%eta_old_north) ! Coupled hydrostatic multi-layer arrays if ( state % use_multilayer ) then !$acc enter data copyin(state%multilayer%dsig_ml, state%multilayer%vcoord_z_ref, & !$acc&                  state%multilayer%h_layer, state%multilayer%hu_layer, state%multilayer%hv_layer, & !$acc&                  state%multilayer%h_layer0, state%multilayer%hu_layer0, state%multilayer%hv_layer0, & !$acc&                  state%multilayer%w_ml, state%multilayer%hu_ml_pre, state%multilayer%hv_ml_pre, & !$acc&                  state%multilayer%rho_layer, state%multilayer%rho_ref) if ( allocated ( state % multilayer % vcoord_z_ref_col )) then ! associate hoists the leaf so the attach path is one-deep ! (ifx OpenMP-target chokes on deep `state%multilayer%X` chains ! in map clauses; enforced by openmp_portability_lint). associate ( vref_col => state % multilayer % vcoord_z_ref_col ) !$acc enter data copyin(vref_col) end associate end if ! KPP boundary-layer workspace (allocated only when the overlay is ! enabled). associate-leaf for the one-deep attach path. if ( allocated ( state % multilayer % kpp_bl_depth )) then associate ( kpp_bl => state % multilayer % kpp_bl_depth , & kpp_us => state % multilayer % kpp_u_star , & kpp_bf => state % multilayer % kpp_buoy_flux ) !$acc enter data copyin(kpp_bl, kpp_us, kpp_bf) end associate end if ! k-epsilon interface-shaped prognostics (allocated only when the ! closure is on). State, not scratch — implicit diffusion reads the ! previous-step nu_t — so copyin and keep device-resident. if ( allocated ( state % multilayer % keps_tke )) then associate ( ke_tke => state % multilayer % keps_tke , & ke_eps => state % multilayer % keps_eps , & ke_nut => state % multilayer % keps_nut , & ke_kt => state % multilayer % keps_kt , & ke_usb => state % multilayer % keps_ustar_b , & ke_uss => state % multilayer % keps_ustar_s ) !$acc enter data copyin(ke_tke, ke_eps, ke_nut, ke_kt, ke_usb, ke_uss) end associate end if if ( allocated ( state % multilayer % bed_sediment )) then associate ( bed_sed => state % multilayer % bed_sediment ) !$acc enter data copyin(bed_sed) end associate end if ! Generic tracer registry (salinity + temperature). Two-step ! attach: array descriptor first (so the device knows the shape of ! tracers(:)), then each element's hTr/hTr0. Without the parent ! copyin, NVHPC stdpar can't dereference tracers(it)%hTr from a ! do-concurrent body. if ( allocated ( state % multilayer % tracers )) then call solver_tracers_enter_data ( state ) end if ! Persistent, caller-owned scratch for the `pure` tracer kernels, ! allocated once up front (never lazily in the per-step kernel). ! Guard each group by what the config actually runs so a PPM-only ! run does not allocate the upwind snapshot (and vice-versa), and ! the hdiff scratch only exists when a tracer diffuses. if ( allocated ( state % multilayer % tracers )) then ! use_ppm_tracer selects the high-order branch; tracer_recon_code ! then picks PPM vs WENO within it, so exactly one of the PPM / ! WENO scratch groups is allocated (never both). want_ppm = state % multilayer % use_ppm_tracer . and . & state % multilayer % tracer_recon_code == TRACER_RECON_PPM want_weno = state % multilayer % use_ppm_tracer . and . & state % multilayer % tracer_recon_code /= TRACER_RECON_PPM want_upwind = . not . state % multilayer % use_ppm_tracer ! Horizontal-diffusion scratch is needed iff the hdiff kernel can ! fire for any tracer.  The kernel's per-step call guard reads the ! PER-TRACER hdiff_kappa, but that is SYNCED from the multilayer ! scalar every step (see solver_step) — at enter_data time the ! per-tracer copy may still be zero while the scalar is already set ! (config sets both; a direct scalar-set, as tests do, sets only the ! scalar).  So gate on the SCALAR (the source of truth, fixed before ! enter_data) plus the static do_horizontal_diffusion flag, never the ! not-yet-synced per-tracer kappa. want_hdiff = . false . if ( state % multilayer % hdiff_kappa > 0.0_wp ) then do it = 1 , size ( state % multilayer % tracers ) if ( state % multilayer % tracers ( it )% do_horizontal_diffusion ) then want_hdiff = . true . end if end do end if call tracer_scratch_ensure ( tracer_scratch , & state % grid % nx_total , state % grid % ny_total , & state % multilayer % nz_ml , & want_ppm , want_upwind , want_hdiff , & want_weno = want_weno ) end if end if ! Non-hydrostatic arrays if ( state % use_nonhydrostatic ) then !$acc enter data copyin(state%nh%dsig, state%nh%u_layer, state%nh%v_layer, & !$acc&                  state%nh%w, state%nh%q_nh, & !$acc&                  state%nh%u_layer0, state%nh%v_layer0, state%nh%w0, & !$acc&                  state%nh%cg_r, state%nh%cg_p, state%nh%cg_Ap, & !$acc&                  state%nh%hu_pre, state%nh%hv_pre) if ( state % nh % nh_preconditioner ) then !$acc enter data copyin(state%nh%cg_z) end if end if ! Mode splitting arrays if ( allocated ( state % nh % h_avg )) then !$acc enter data create(state%nh%h_avg, state%nh%hu_avg, state%nh%hv_avg, & !$acc&                  state%nh%h_bt0, state%nh%hu_bt0, state%nh%hv_bt0, & !$acc&                  state%nh%baro_tend_x, state%nh%baro_tend_y) end if end subroutine solver_enter_data","tags":"","url":"proc/solver_enter_data.html"},{"title":"solver_exit_data – Rakali","text":"public  subroutine solver_exit_data(state) Copy state arrays back from GPU device. Called once at simulation\nend or for final output. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( state_t ), intent(inout) :: state Solution state to retrieve from device Calls proc~~solver_exit_data~~CallsGraph proc~solver_exit_data solver_exit_data proc~ml_dynamics_workspaces_cleanup ml_dynamics_workspaces_cleanup proc~solver_exit_data->proc~ml_dynamics_workspaces_cleanup proc~ml_horizontal_viscosity_cleanup ml_horizontal_viscosity_cleanup proc~solver_