is Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a procedure to one which it calls. Dashed \narrows point from an interface to procedures which implement that interface.\nThis could include the module procedures in a generic interface or the\nimplementation in a submodule of an interface in a parent module. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Variables Type Visibility Attributes Name Initial integer, private :: din integer, private :: dit integer, private :: djn integer, private :: djt real(kind=wp), private :: h_int integer, private :: ig integer, private :: ig0 integer, private :: ii real(kind=wp), private :: in_sgn integer, private :: jg integer, private :: jg0 integer, private :: ji integer, private :: k integer, private :: n_tan integer, private :: nghost logical, private :: nu integer, private :: t Source Code pure subroutine bc_inflow_edge ( h , hu , hv , b , grid , ed , vel ) !! Inflow: zero-gradient ghost depth; the NORMAL momentum is the !! prescribed inward transport h_ghost*vel (inward = -ed%nrm_sign); !! tangential momentum copied from the first interior cell. type ( hgrid_t ), intent ( in ) :: grid real ( wp ), intent ( inout ) :: h ( grid % nx_total , grid % ny_total ) real ( wp ), intent ( inout ) :: hu ( grid % nx_total , grid % ny_total ) real ( wp ), intent ( inout ) :: hv ( grid % nx_total , grid % ny_total ) real ( wp ), intent ( in ) :: b ( grid % nx_total , grid % ny_total ) type ( edge_t ), intent ( in ) :: ed real ( wp ), intent ( in ) :: vel integer :: t , k , ig , jg , ii , ji , nghost integer :: ig0 , jg0 , din , djn , dit , djt , n_tan real ( wp ) :: in_sgn , h_int logical :: nu nghost = grid % nghost ig0 = ed % ig0 jg0 = ed % jg0 din = ed % di_n djn = ed % dj_n dit = ed % di_t djt = ed % dj_t n_tan = ed % n_tan nu = ed % normal_is_u in_sgn = - ed % nrm_sign do concurrent ( t = 1 : n_tan , k = 1 : nghost ) local ( ig , jg , ii , ji , h_int ) ig = ig0 + ( k - 1 ) * din + ( t - 1 ) * dit jg = jg0 + ( k - 1 ) * djn + ( t - 1 ) * djt ii = ig0 + ( t - 1 ) * dit - din ji = jg0 + ( t - 1 ) * djt - djn h_int = h ( ii , ji ) h ( ig , jg ) = h_int if ( nu ) then hu ( ig , jg ) = in_sgn * h_int * vel hv ( ig , jg ) = hv ( ii , ji ) else hv ( ig , jg ) = in_sgn * h_int * vel hu ( ig , jg ) = hu ( ii , ji ) end if end do end subroutine bc_inflow_edge","tags":"","url":"proc/bc_inflow_edge.html"},{"title":"bc_wall_edge – Rakali","text":"private pure subroutine bc_wall_edge(h, hu, hv, b, grid, ed) Wall / sponge ghost fill for one edge: mirror the BED and the\nsurface, negate the NORMAL momentum, copy the tangential.\nGeometry comes from ed ; its scalars are hoisted to locals so the\ndo concurrent carries no derived-type dereference. Why the bed is mirrored too A wall IS a reflection: the ghost column is a fictitious mirror of\nthe interior, so its bed must equal its mirror’s bed.  Anything else\ndescribes a domain that CONTINUES past the wall with different\ntopography — which is not a wall. Formula bathymetry setters fill the ghost rows by evaluating the\nformula there (they must, or the EOS sees h_layer = 0 at ghosts and\nfalls back to rho_0), so without this line b(ghost) is the\nCONTINUATION of the topography rather than its reflection, and the\nreconstruction across the wall face is asymmetric. At rest that is harmless — the Audusse hydrostatic reconstruction\nstill returns exactly zero flux, which is why the C-property held to\n1e-12 over irregular bathymetry.  But WITH FLOW the asymmetry gives a\nnon-zero HLL mass flux through a closed wall.  Measured on a closed\nbox, stratified, sinusoidal bed, 400 steps: relative mass change\n1.364e-6 with the formula bed in the ghosts vs -2.1e-14 with it\nmirrored, and the solver’s own mass_out accumulator attributed\n100 % of it to boundary flux (budget residual 2e-14). Mirroring here rather than once at setup means it also survives a bed\nthat EVOLVES — which is exactly what the Exner update in the\nmorphodynamics plan will do. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name real(kind=wp), intent(inout) :: h (grid%nx_total,grid%ny_total) real(kind=wp), intent(inout) :: hu (grid%nx_total,grid%ny_total) real(kind=wp), intent(inout) :: hv (grid%nx_total,grid%ny_total) real(kind=wp), intent(inout) :: b (grid%nx_total,grid%ny_total) type( hgrid_t ), intent(in) :: grid type( edge_t ), intent(in) :: ed Calls proc~~bc_wall_edge~~CallsGraph proc~bc_wall_edge bc_wall_edge local local proc~bc_wall_edge->local Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Interface Interface Type Bound Procedure Type Bound Procedure Unknown Procedure Type Unknown Procedure Type Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a procedure to one which it calls. Dashed \narrows point from an interface to procedures which implement that interface.\nThis could include the module procedures in a generic interface or the\nimplementation in a submodule of an interface in a parent module. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Called by proc~~bc_wall_edge~~CalledByGraph proc~bc_wall_edge bc_wall_edge proc~apply_boundary_conditions apply_boundary_conditions proc~apply_boundary_conditions->proc~bc_wall_edge proc~solver_advance_2d_generic solver_advance_2d_generic proc~solver_advance_2d_generic->proc~apply_boundary_conditions proc~solver_barotropic_rk_stage solver_barotropic_rk_stage proc~solver_barotropic_rk_stage->proc~apply_boundary_conditions proc~solver_multilayer_rk_stage solver_multilayer_rk_stage proc~solver_multilayer_rk_stage->proc~apply_boundary_conditions proc~solver_multilayer_rk_stage_nz1 solver_multilayer_rk_stage_nz1 proc~solver_multilayer_rk_stage->proc~solver_multilayer_rk_stage_nz1 proc~solver_multilayer_rk_stage_nz1->proc~apply_boundary_conditions proc~solver_nh_rk_stage solver_nh_rk_stage proc~solver_nh_rk_stage->proc~apply_boundary_conditions proc~solver_step_impl solver_step_impl proc~solver_step_impl->proc~apply_boundary_conditions proc~solver_step_impl->proc~solver_advance_2d_generic proc~solver_step_impl->proc~solver_nh_rk_stage proc~solver_step_multilayer_impl solver_step_multilayer_impl proc~solver_step_multilayer_impl->proc~apply_boundary_conditions proc~solver_step_multilayer_impl->proc~solver_multilayer_rk_stage proc~solver_step_split_impl solver_step_split_impl proc~solver_step_split_impl->proc~apply_boundary_conditions proc~solver_step_split_impl->proc~solver_barotropic_rk_stage proc~solver_step solver_step proc~solver_step->proc~solver_step_impl proc~solver_step->proc~solver_step_multilayer_impl proc~solver_step->proc~solver_step_split_impl proc~driver_run_coastal driver_run_coastal proc~driver_run_coastal->proc~solver_step proc~rki_advance rki_advance proc~rki_advance->proc~solver_step proc~rki_step rki_step proc~rki_step->proc~solver_step proc~driver_run driver_run proc~driver_run->proc~driver_run_coastal Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Interface Interface Type Bound Procedure Type Bound Procedure Unknown Procedure Type Unknown Procedure Type Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a procedure to one which it calls. Dashed \narrows point from an interface to procedures which implement that interface.\nThis could include the module procedures in a generic interface or the\nimplementation in a submodule of an interface in a parent module. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Variables Type Visibility Attributes Name Initial integer, private :: din integer, private :: dit integer, private :: djn integer, private :: djt integer, private :: ig integer, private :: ig0 integer, private :: im integer, private :: jg integer, private :: jg0 integer, private :: jm integer, private :: k integer, private :: n_tan integer, private :: nghost logical, private :: nu integer, private :: t Source Code pure subroutine bc_wall_edge ( h , hu , hv , b , grid , ed ) !! Wall / sponge ghost fill for one edge: mirror the BED and the !! surface, negate the NORMAL momentum, copy the tangential. !! Geometry comes from `ed`; its scalars are hoisted to locals so the !! do concurrent carries no derived-type dereference. !! !! ## Why the bed is mirrored too !! !! A wall IS a reflection: the ghost column is a fictitious mirror of !! the interior, so its bed must equal its mirror's bed.  Anything else !! describes a domain that CONTINUES past the wall with different !! topography — which is not a wall. !! !! Formula bathymetry setters fill the ghost rows by evaluating the !! formula there (they must, or the EOS sees h_layer = 0 at ghosts and !! falls back to rho_0), so without this line `b(ghost)` is the !! CONTINUATION of the topography rather than its reflection, and the !! reconstruction across the wall face is asymmetric. !! !! At rest that is harmless — the Audusse hydrostatic reconstruction !! still returns exactly zero flux, which is why the C-property held to !! 1e-12 over irregular bathymetry.  But WITH FLOW the asymmetry gives a !! non-zero HLL mass flux through a closed wall.  Measured on a closed !! box, stratified, sinusoidal bed, 400 steps: relative mass change !! 1.364e-6 with the formula bed in the ghosts vs -2.1e-14 with it !! mirrored, and the solver's own `mass_out` accumulator attributed !! 100 % of it to boundary flux (budget residual 2e-14). !! !! Mirroring here rather than once at setup means it also survives a bed !! that EVOLVES — which is exactly what the Exner update in the !! morphodynamics plan will do. type ( hgrid_t ), intent ( in ) :: grid real ( wp ), intent ( inout ) :: h ( grid % nx_total , grid % ny_total ) real ( wp ), intent ( inout ) :: hu ( grid % nx_total , grid % ny_total ) real ( wp ), intent ( inout ) :: hv ( grid % nx_total , grid % ny_total ) real ( wp ), intent ( inout ) :: b ( grid % nx_total , grid % ny_total ) type ( edge_t ), intent ( in ) :: ed integer :: t , k , ig , jg , im , jm , nghost integer :: ig0 , jg0 , din , djn , dit , djt , n_tan logical :: nu nghost = grid % nghost ig0 = ed % ig0 jg0 = ed % jg0 din = ed % di_n djn = ed % dj_n dit = ed % di_t djt = ed % dj_t n_tan = ed % n_tan nu = ed % normal_is_u do concurrent ( t = 1 : n_tan , k = 1 : nghost ) local ( ig , jg , im , jm ) ig = ig0 + ( k - 1 ) * din + ( t - 1 ) * dit ! ghost cell jg = jg0 + ( k - 1 ) * djn + ( t - 1 ) * djt im = ig - ( 2 * k - 1 ) * din ! mirror interior cell jm = jg - ( 2 * k - 1 ) * djn ! Mirror the bed FIRST (see the note above), then the surface.  With ! b symmetric the eta-mirror below reduces to h(ig,jg) = h(im,jm); ! the general form is kept so the routine stays correct if a caller ! ever supplies an already-mirrored bed. b ( ig , jg ) = b ( im , jm ) h ( ig , jg ) = max ( 0.0_wp , h ( im , jm ) + b ( im , jm ) - b ( ig , jg )) if ( nu ) then hu ( ig , jg ) = - hu ( im , jm ) hv ( ig , jg ) = hv ( im , jm ) else hv ( ig , jg ) = - hv ( im , jm ) hu ( ig , jg ) = hu ( im , jm ) end if end do end subroutine bc_wall_edge","tags":"","url":"proc/bc_wall_edge.html"},{"title":"fill_bathymetry_ghosts_array – Rakali","text":"public  subroutine fill_bathymetry_ghosts_array(b, grid) Fill ghost cell bathymetry by constant extrapolation from the\nnearest interior cell.  This ensures boundary flux computations\nsee a consistent bottom elevation across the ghost-interior\ninterface.  Works on a target 2D array directly so both the\ncoastal and ocean barotropic slots can reuse it. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name real(kind=wp), intent(inout) :: b (:,:) type( hgrid_t ), intent(in) :: grid Called by proc~~fill_bathymetry_ghosts_array~~CalledByGraph proc~fill_bathymetry_ghosts_array fill_bathymetry_ghosts_array proc~fill_bathymetry_ghosts fill_bathymetry_ghosts proc~fill_bathymetry_ghosts->proc~fill_bathymetry_ghosts_array proc~load_bathymetry_into_array load_bathymetry_into_array proc~load_bathymetry_into_array->proc~fill_bathymetry_ghosts_array proc~init_local_state init_local_state proc~init_local_state->proc~fill_bathymetry_ghosts proc~load_bathymetry load_bathymetry proc~init_local_state->proc~load_bathymetry proc~load_bathymetry->proc~load_bathymetry_into_array proc~rki_finalize rki_finalize proc~rki_finalize->proc~fill_bathymetry_ghosts proc~rki_finalize->proc~load_bathymetry proc~rki_set_bathymetry rki_set_bathymetry proc~rki_set_bathymetry->proc~fill_bathymetry_ghosts proc~driver_run_coastal driver_run_coastal proc~driver_run_coastal->proc~init_local_state proc~rki_create_from_namelist rki_create_from_namelist proc~rki_create_from_namelist->proc~rki_finalize proc~rki_create_from_namelist_string rki_create_from_namelist_string proc~rki_create_from_namelist_string->proc~rki_finalize proc~driver_run driver_run proc~driver_run->proc~driver_run_coastal Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Interface Interface Type Bound Procedure Type Bound Procedure Unknown Procedure Type Unknown Procedure Type Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a procedure to one which it calls. Dashed \narrows point from an interface to procedures which implement that interface.\nThis could include the module procedures in a generic interface or the\nimplementation in a submodule of an interface in a parent module. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Variables Type Visibility Attributes Name Initial integer, private :: i integer, private :: j integer, private :: ng integer, private :: nx integer, private :: ny Source Code subroutine fill_bathymetry_ghosts_array ( b , grid ) !! Fill ghost cell bathymetry by constant extrapolation from the !! nearest interior cell.  This ensures boundary flux computations !! see a consistent bottom elevation across the ghost-interior !! interface.  Works on a target 2D array directly so both the !! coastal and ocean barotropic slots can reuse it. real ( wp ), intent ( inout ) :: b (:, :) type ( hgrid_t ), intent ( in ) :: grid integer :: ng , i , j , nx , ny ng = grid % nghost nx = grid % nx_phys ny = grid % ny_phys ! West and east ghost columns do j = 1 , grid % ny_total do i = 1 , ng b ( i , j ) = b ( ng + 1 , j ) ! west b ( ng + nx + i , j ) = b ( ng + nx , j ) ! east end do end do ! South and north ghost rows (corners already filled above) do j = 1 , ng do i = 1 , grid % nx_total b ( i , j ) = b ( i , ng + 1 ) ! south b ( i , ng + ny + j ) = b ( i , ng + ny ) ! north end do end do end subroutine fill_bathymetry_ghosts_array","tags":"","url":"proc/fill_bathymetry_ghosts_array.html"},{"title":"load_bathymetry_into_array – Rakali","text":"public  subroutine load_bathymetry_into_array(filename, b, grid) Load bathymetry from a NetCDF file into a target 2D array\nshaped (grid%nx_total, grid%ny_total) . The file must contain:\n  - Dimensions: x (nx_phys), y (ny_phys)\n  - Variable: b(x, y) or elevation(x, y) or depth(x, y) Handles NetCDF C/Fortran dimension reversal: files written by\nPython/C store b(x,y) in C order, which Fortran reads as b(y,x).\nIf the direct dimensions don’t match but the transposed ones do,\nwe read transposed and copy correctly. Bathymetry lands in the interior; ghost cells are filled by\nconstant extrapolation from the nearest interior cell via fill_bathymetry_ghosts_array . Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name character(len=*), intent(in) :: filename real(kind=wp), intent(inout) :: b (:,:) type( hgrid_t ), intent(in) :: grid Calls proc~~load_bathymetry_into_array~~CallsGraph proc~load_bathymetry_into_array load_bathymetry_into_array error error proc~load_bathymetry_into_array->error info info proc~load_bathymetry_into_array->info nf90_inquire_dimension nf90_inquire_dimension proc~load_bathymetry_into_array->nf90_inquire_dimension nf90_inquire_variable nf90_inquire_variable proc~load_bathymetry_into_array->nf90_inquire_variable proc~fill_bathymetry_ghosts_array fill_bathymetry_ghosts_array proc~load_bathymetry_into_array->proc~fill_bathymetry_ghosts_array proc~nc_check nc_check proc~load_bathymetry_into_array->proc~nc_check proc~nc_close nc_close proc~load_bathymetry_into_array->proc~nc_close proc~nc_get_dim_len nc_get_dim_len proc~load_bathymetry_into_array->proc~nc_get_dim_len proc~nc_get_var_2d nc_get_var_2d proc~load_bathymetry_into_array->proc~nc_get_var_2d proc~nc_open_read nc_open_read proc~load_bathymetry_into_array->proc~nc_open_read proc~try_get_bathymetry_var try_get_bathymetry_var proc~load_bathymetry_into_array->proc~try_get_bathymetry_var to_string to_string proc~load_bathymetry_into_array->to_string proc~nc_check->error nf90_strerror nf90_strerror proc~nc_check->nf90_strerror proc~nc_close->proc~nc_check nf90_close nf90_close proc~nc_close->nf90_close proc~nc_get_dim_len->nf90_inquire_dimension proc~nc_get_dim_len->proc~nc_check nf90_inq_dimid nf90_inq_dimid proc~nc_get_dim_len->nf90_inq_dimid proc~nc_get_var_2d->proc~nc_check nf90_get_var nf90_get_var proc~nc_get_var_2d->nf90_get_var proc~nc_open_read->proc~nc_check nf90_open nf90_open proc~nc_open_read->nf90_open proc~try_get_bathymetry_var->error nf90_inq_varid nf90_inq_varid proc~try_get_bathymetry_var->nf90_inq_varid Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Interface Interface Type Bound Procedure Type Bound Procedure Unknown Procedure Type Unknown Procedure Type Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a procedure to one which it calls. Dashed \narrows point from an interface to procedures which implement that interface.\nThis could include the module procedures in a generic interface or the\nimplementation in a submodule of an interface in a parent module. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Called by proc~~load_bathymetry_into_array~~CalledByGraph proc~load_bathymetry_into_array load_bathymetry_into_array proc~load_bathymetry load_bathymetry proc~load_bathymetry->proc~load_bathymetry_into_array proc~init_local_state init_local_state proc~init_local_state->proc~load_bathymetry proc~rki_finalize rki_finalize proc~rki_finalize->proc~load_bathymetry proc~driver_run_coastal driver_run_coastal proc~driver_run_coastal->proc~init_local_state proc~rki_create_from_namelist rki_create_from_namelist proc~rki_create_from_namelist->proc~rki_finalize proc~rki_create_from_namelist_string rki_create_from_namelist_string proc~rki_create_from_namelist_string->proc~rki_finalize proc~driver_run driver_run proc~driver_run->proc~driver_run_coastal Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Interface Interface Type Bound Procedure Type Bound Procedure Unknown Procedure Type Unknown Procedure Type Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a procedure to one which it calls. Dashed \narrows point from an interface to procedures which implement that interface.\nThis could include the module procedures in a generic interface or the\nimplementation in a submodule of an interface in a parent module. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Variables Type Visibility Attributes Name Initial real(kind=wp), private, allocatable :: b_interior (:,:) integer, private :: dim1_len character(len=64), private :: dim1_name integer, private :: dim2_len character(len=64), private :: dim2_name integer, private :: file_nx integer, private :: file_ny integer, private :: i integer, private :: j integer, private :: ncid logical, private :: needs_transpose integer, private :: ng integer, private :: status integer, private :: var_dimids (2) integer, private :: var_ndims integer, private :: varid Source Code subroutine load_bathymetry_into_array ( filename , b , grid ) !! Load bathymetry from a NetCDF file into a target 2D array !! shaped `(grid%nx_total, grid%ny_total)`. !! !! The file must contain: !!   - Dimensions: x (nx_phys), y (ny_phys) !!   - Variable: b(x, y) or elevation(x, y) or depth(x, y) !! !! Handles NetCDF C/Fortran dimension reversal: files written by !! Python/C store b(x,y) in C order, which Fortran reads as b(y,x). !! If the direct dimensions don't match but the transposed ones do, !! we read transposed and copy correctly. !! !! Bathymetry lands in the interior; ghost cells are filled by !! constant extrapolation from the nearest interior cell via !! `fill_bathymetry_ghosts_array`. character ( len =* ), intent ( in ) :: filename real ( wp ), intent ( inout ) :: b (:, :) type ( hgrid_t ), intent ( in ) :: grid integer :: ncid , varid , file_nx , file_ny integer :: ng , i , j integer :: var_dimids ( 2 ) integer :: var_ndims , dim1_len , dim2_len integer :: status character ( len = 64 ) :: dim1_name , dim2_name logical :: needs_transpose real ( wp ), allocatable :: b_interior (:, :) call logger % info ( \"Loading bathymetry from: \" // trim ( filename )) call nc_open_read ( filename , ncid ) ! Read grid dimensions (by name — these are the logical sizes) call nc_get_dim_len ( ncid , \"x\" , file_nx ) call nc_get_dim_len ( ncid , \"y\" , file_ny ) ! Try variable names in order: b, elevation, depth call try_get_bathymetry_var ( ncid , varid ) ! Query the variable's actual dimension order in the file. ! Files written by Python/C store b(x,y) in C-order. Fortran's ! NetCDF library reverses this, so the variable appears as b(y,x) ! in Fortran. We detect this by checking the first dimension name. call nc_check ( nf90_inquire_variable ( ncid , varid , ndims = var_ndims , & dimids = var_dimids ), \"querying bathymetry variable\" ) call nc_check ( nf90_inquire_dimension ( ncid , var_dimids ( 1 ), & name = dim1_name , len = dim1_len ), \"querying dim 1\" ) call nc_check ( nf90_inquire_dimension ( ncid , var_dimids ( 2 ), & name = dim2_name , len = dim2_len ), \"querying dim 2\" ) call logger % info ( \"Bathymetry variable: \" // trim ( dim1_name ) // \"=\" // & to_string ( dim1_len ) // \" x \" // trim ( dim2_name ) // \"=\" // & to_string ( dim2_len ) // \" (Fortran order)\" ) ! In Fortran, dim1 is the first array index (varies fastest). ! If dim1 is \"x\" and matches nx, no transpose needed. ! If dim1 is \"y\" (C-order file reversed), we need to transpose. needs_transpose = ( trim ( dim1_name ) == \"y\" ) ! Validate dimensions if ( needs_transpose ) then if ( dim1_len /= grid % ny_phys . or . dim2_len /= grid % nx_phys ) then call logger % error ( \"Bathymetry grid mismatch: file has \" // & to_string ( dim2_len ) // \" x \" // to_string ( dim1_len ) // & \" but simulation expects \" // & to_string ( grid % nx_phys ) // \" x \" // & to_string ( grid % ny_phys )) call nc_close ( ncid ) error stop \"Bathymetry grid mismatch\" end if else if ( dim1_len /= grid % nx_phys . or . dim2_len /= grid % ny_phys ) then call logger % error ( \"Bathymetry grid mismatch: file has \" // & to_string ( dim1_len ) // \" x \" // to_string ( dim2_len ) // & \" but simulation expects \" // & to_string ( grid % nx_phys ) // \" x \" // & to_string ( grid % ny_phys )) call nc_close ( ncid ) error stop \"Bathymetry grid mismatch\" end if end if ng = grid % nghost ! Read into temporary array matching the Fortran storage order allocate ( b_interior ( dim1_len , dim2_len )) call nc_get_var_2d ( ncid , varid , b_interior ) call nc_close ( ncid ) if ( needs_transpose ) then ! b_interior is (y, x) in Fortran — transpose to (x, y) for b do j = 1 , grid % ny_phys do i = 1 , grid % nx_phys b ( ng + i , ng + j ) = b_interior ( j , i ) end do end do else b ( ng + 1 : ng + grid % nx_phys , ng + 1 : ng + grid % ny_phys ) = b_interior end if deallocate ( b_interior ) ! Fill ghost cells by constant extrapolation from nearest interior cell. ! Without this, ghost cells retain b=0 which creates artificial cliffs ! against real bathymetry (e.g. b=-50m interior vs b=0 ghost), generating ! extreme velocities and tiny CFL timesteps. call fill_bathymetry_ghosts_array ( b , grid ) call logger % info ( \"Bathymetry loaded: min = \" // & to_string ( minval ( b ( ng + 1 : ng + grid % nx_phys , & ng + 1 : ng + grid % ny_phys ))) // & \" m, max = \" // & to_string ( maxval ( b ( ng + 1 : ng + grid % nx_phys , & ng + 1 : ng + grid % ny_phys ))) // & \" m\" ) end subroutine load_bathymetry_into_array","tags":"","url":"proc/load_bathymetry_into_array.html"},{"title":"try_get_bathymetry_var – Rakali","text":"private  subroutine try_get_bathymetry_var(ncid, varid) Uses netcdf proc~~try_get_bathymetry_var~~UsesGraph proc~try_get_bathymetry_var try_get_bathymetry_var netcdf netcdf proc~try_get_bathymetry_var->netcdf Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Module Module Submodule Submodule Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a submodule to the (sub)module which it is\ndescended from. Dashed arrows point from a module or program unit to \nmodules which it uses. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Try to find the bathymetry variable by common names Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name integer, intent(in) :: ncid integer, intent(out) :: varid Calls proc~~try_get_bathymetry_var~~CallsGraph proc~try_get_bathymetry_var try_get_bathymetry_var error error proc~try_get_bathymetry_var->error nf90_inq_varid nf90_inq_varid proc~try_get_bathymetry_var->nf90_inq_varid Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Interface Interface Type Bound Procedure Type Bound Procedure Unknown Procedure Type Unknown Procedure Type Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a procedure to one which it calls. Dashed \narrows point from an interface to procedures which implement that interface.\nThis could include the module procedures in a generic interface or the\nimplementation in a submodule of an interface in a parent module. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Called by proc~~try_get_bathymetry_var~~CalledByGraph proc~try_get_bathymetry_var try_get_bathymetry_var proc~load_bathymetry_into_array load_bathymetry_into_array proc~load_bathymetry_into_array->proc~try_get_bathymetry_var proc~load_bathymetry load_bathymetry proc~load_bathymetry->proc~load_bathymetry_into_array proc~init_local_state init_local_state proc~init_local_state->proc~load_bathymetry proc~rki_finalize rki_finalize proc~rki_finalize->proc~load_bathymetry proc~driver_run_coastal driver_run_coastal proc~driver_run_coastal->proc~init_local_state proc~rki_create_from_namelist rki_create_from_namelist proc~rki_create_from_namelist->proc~rki_finalize proc~rki_create_from_namelist_string rki_create_from_namelist_string proc~rki_create_from_namelist_string->proc~rki_finalize proc~driver_run driver_run proc~driver_run->proc~driver_run_coastal Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Interface Interface Type Bound Procedure Type Bound Procedure Unknown Procedure Type Unknown Procedure Type Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a procedure to one which it calls. Dashed \narrows point from an interface to procedures which implement that interface.\nThis could include the module procedures in a generic interface or the\nimplementation in a submodule of an interface in a parent module. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Variables Type Visibility Attributes Name Initial integer, private :: status Source Code subroutine try_get_bathymetry_var ( ncid , varid ) !! Try to find the bathymetry variable by common names use netcdf , only : nf90_noerr , nf90_inq_varid integer , intent ( in ) :: ncid integer , intent ( out ) :: varid integer :: status ! Try \"b\" first (our convention) status = nf90_inq_varid ( ncid , \"b\" , varid ) if ( status == nf90_noerr ) return ! Try \"elevation\" status = nf90_inq_varid ( ncid , \"elevation\" , varid ) if ( status == nf90_noerr ) return ! Try \"depth\" status = nf90_inq_varid ( ncid , \"depth\" , varid ) if ( status == nf90_noerr ) return ! None found call logger % error ( \"No bathymetry variable found (tried: b, elevation, depth)\" ) end subroutine try_get_bathymetry_var","tags":"","url":"proc/try_get_bathymetry_var.html"},{"title":"ml_advect_tracer_muscl_unstr – Rakali","text":"public  subroutine ml_advect_tracer_muscl_unstr(state, mesh, hTr_layer, dt, bc_tr_ext) Second-order MUSCL horizontal tracer advection on triangles. Uses the same CWC-consistent per-layer mass flux as the\nfirst-order scheme, but replaces the piecewise-constant upwind\nconcentration with a linearly reconstructed value: Tr_edge = Tr_cell + phi * grad(Tr) . (x_edge - x_centroid) where grad(Tr) is the Green-Gauss gradient on the triangle and\nphi is the Barth-Jespersen limiter that prevents new extrema. Two-phase approach:\n  Phase 1 — Gradient + limiter: compute limited gradient for\n            every cell (owned + halo) so that Phase 2 can read\n            neighbour gradients.\n  Phase 2 — MUSCL advection: same CWC flux construction as the\n            first-order kernel, but upwind concentration is the\n            limited edge-extrapolated value. Cell-centric double-visit pattern preserved: each owned cell\nwrites only to its own hTr_layer column. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( unstr_state_t ), intent(inout) :: state type( mesh_unstr_t ), intent(in) :: mesh real(kind=wp), intent(inout), contiguous :: hTr_layer (:,:) real(kind=wp), intent(in) :: dt real(kind=wp), intent(in), optional :: bc_tr_ext (MAX_BC_GROUPS) Calls proc~~ml_advect_tracer_muscl_unstr~~CallsGraph proc~ml_advect_tracer_muscl_unstr ml_advect_tracer_muscl_unstr proc~ml_advect_tracer_muscl_unstr_impl ml_advect_tracer_muscl_unstr_impl proc~ml_advect_tracer_muscl_unstr->proc~ml_advect_tracer_muscl_unstr_impl local local proc~ml_advect_tracer_muscl_unstr_impl->local Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Interface Interface Type Bound Procedure Type Bound Procedure Unknown Procedure Type Unknown Procedure Type Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a procedure to one which it calls. Dashed \narrows point from an interface to procedures which implement that interface.\nThis could include the module procedures in a generic interface or the\nimplementation in a submodule of an interface in a parent module. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Called by proc~~ml_advect_tracer_muscl_unstr~~CalledByGraph proc~ml_advect_tracer_muscl_unstr ml_advect_tracer_muscl_unstr proc~solver_unstr_step solver_unstr_step proc~solver_unstr_step->proc~ml_advect_tracer_muscl_unstr proc~rki_unstr_step rki_unstr_step proc~rki_unstr_step->proc~solver_unstr_step proc~solver_unstr_advance solver_unstr_advance proc~solver_unstr_advance->proc~solver_unstr_step proc~driver_run_coastal_unstr driver_run_coastal_unstr proc~driver_run_coastal_unstr->proc~solver_unstr_advance proc~rki_unstr_advance rki_unstr_advance proc~rki_unstr_advance->proc~solver_unstr_advance proc~driver_run driver_run proc~driver_run->proc~driver_run_coastal_unstr Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Interface Interface Type Bound Procedure Type Bound Procedure Unknown Procedure Type Unknown Procedure Type Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a procedure to one which it calls. Dashed \narrows point from an interface to procedures which implement that interface.\nThis could include the module procedures in a generic interface or the\nimplementation in a submodule of an interface in a parent module. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Variables Type Visibility Attributes Name Initial real(kind=wp), private :: bc_ext_local (MAX_BC_GROUPS) Source Code subroutine ml_advect_tracer_muscl_unstr ( state , mesh , hTr_layer , dt , bc_tr_ext ) !! Second-order MUSCL horizontal tracer advection on triangles. !! !! Uses the same CWC-consistent per-layer mass flux as the !! first-order scheme, but replaces the piecewise-constant upwind !! concentration with a linearly reconstructed value: !! !!   Tr_edge = Tr_cell + phi * grad(Tr) . (x_edge - x_centroid) !! !! where grad(Tr) is the Green-Gauss gradient on the triangle and !! phi is the Barth-Jespersen limiter that prevents new extrema. !! !! Two-phase approach: !!   Phase 1 — Gradient + limiter: compute limited gradient for !!             every cell (owned + halo) so that Phase 2 can read !!             neighbour gradients. !!   Phase 2 — MUSCL advection: same CWC flux construction as the !!             first-order kernel, but upwind concentration is the !!             limited edge-extrapolated value. !! !! Cell-centric double-visit pattern preserved: each owned cell !! writes only to its own hTr_layer column. type ( unstr_state_t ), intent ( inout ) :: state type ( mesh_unstr_t ), intent ( in ) :: mesh real ( wp ), intent ( inout ), contiguous :: hTr_layer (:, :) real ( wp ), intent ( in ) :: dt real ( wp ), intent ( in ), optional :: bc_tr_ext ( MAX_BC_GROUPS ) real ( wp ) :: bc_ext_local ( MAX_BC_GROUPS ) if (. not . state % use_multilayer ) return if ( present ( bc_tr_ext )) then bc_ext_local = bc_tr_ext else bc_ext_local = 0.0_wp end if ! Outer shim: forward the state/mesh array components to the flat _impl ! as explicit-shape dummies, so the device kernels address contiguous raw ! pointers instead of derived-type descriptors.  Descriptor access drags ! base+strides+bounds into registers on every reference (144 regs/thread ! -> 18.75% occupancy on this kernel); explicit-shape dummies cut that. call ml_advect_tracer_muscl_unstr_impl ( & state % multilayer % nz_ml , state % ncells_total , mesh % ncells_owned , & state % multilayer % h_layer , state % multilayer % hu_layer , & state % multilayer % hv_layer , state % multilayer % dsig_ml , & state % multilayer % tracer_advect_scratch , & state % multilayer % ml_lgrad_x , state % multilayer % ml_lgrad_y , & state % barotropic % mass_flux_e , mesh % area , mesh % neighbours , & mesh % surrogate_neighbours , mesh % edge_n1 , mesh % edge_n2 , & mesh % edge_len , mesh % edge_mid_x , mesh % edge_mid_y , & mesh % centroid_x , mesh % centroid_y , mesh % bc_group , & hTr_layer , dt , bc_ext_local ) end subroutine ml_advect_tracer_muscl_unstr","tags":"","url":"proc/ml_advect_tracer_muscl_unstr.html"},{"title":"ml_advect_tracer_unstr – Rakali","text":"public pure subroutine ml_advect_tracer_unstr(state, mesh, hTr_layer, dt, bc_tr_ext) First-order upwind horizontal tracer advection on triangles\nusing a SHEAR-AWARE consistency-with-continuity (CWC) per-layer\nmass flux. Mirrors what the structured ml_advect_tracer does\nwith its 0.5*(hu_layer left + right) face flux, but reshaped\nfor the cell-centric triangle pattern. Per-layer mass flux at an interior edge:\n  v_layer(k) = -(hu_edge_k * n1 + hv_edge_k * n2) * L_e\n    where hu_edge_k = 0.5 * (hu_layer(k, here) + hu_layer(k, nbr))\n  correction = mass_flux_e - sum_k v_layer(k)\n  f_layer(k) = v_layer(k) + dsig_k * correction The construction enforces sum_k f_layer(k) = mass_flux_e exactly, so per-cell tracer mass conservation matches the\nbarotropic continuity update. The distribution between\nlayers reflects the actual per-layer momentum, so a buoyant\nplume (surface seaward / bottom return flow) advects tracer\nin the right direction at every layer. For depth-uniform velocity (no baroclinic shear) v_layer(k) =\ndsig_k * sum_k v_layer and the correction term cancels the\ncell-avg-edge-vs-CU-flux mismatch exactly, so the scheme\nreduces to f_layer(k) = dsig_k * mass_flux_e . Uniform\ntracer is preserved bit-perfectly when there is no shear,\nwhich is the property test_tracer_uniform_stays_uniform checks (it gates on beta_S = alpha_T = 0 ). For boundary edges we fall back to f_layer(k) = dsig_k *\nmass_flux_e (no neighbour to average against; the BC kernel\nhas already prescribed the barotropic mass flux there). Known limitation: per-cell mass conservation is exact, but\nper-layer hS evolution does not exactly track per-layer dsig_k * H_new evolution under shear. As a result, the\nscheme can produce small bounded over- or undershoots\n(typically << 1 PSU at sharp baroclinic fronts). The S_min / S_max clamp in ml_clamp_tracer_unstr is the\nsafety net. The proper fix is an ALE-style remap of hS\nbetween the per-layer-advected mass and the sigma h after\ndistribute – not landing in this PR. Boundary handling:\n  - Wall edges: CU flux kernel writes mass_flux_e = 0, so no\n    tracer flux through walls automatically.\n  - Inflow edges (mass_flux_e > 0 at boundary): use bc_tr_ext\n    indexed by the edge’s boundary group.\n  - Outflow edges: upwind is interior cell, handled normally. Cell-centric double-visit pattern preserved: each owned\ntriangle writes only into its own hTr_layer column. The\npair of visits on interior edges produces equal-and-opposite\ncontributions because mass_flux_e(e, K) = -mass_flux_e(e',\nN) across a shared edge and both sides see the same upwind\ntracer value. Jacobi-vs-Gauss-Seidel note: we snapshot the input hTr_layer into state%multilayer%tracer_advect_scratch on entry and have the\nkernel body read from the scratch for BOTH the own-cell and\nneighbour-cell concentrations, writing only to hTr_layer .\nWithout this, the enclosing do concurrent over owned cells\nproduces correct results on GPU (launch-time Jacobi semantics)\nbut biased results on CPU, where do concurrent collapses to\na sequential / OMP loop and later cell iterations observe\nalready-updated neighbour values. The result is a drift of\n~3e-6 per step on a uniform tracer that compounds over time. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( unstr_state_t ), intent(inout) :: state type( mesh_unstr_t ), intent(in) :: mesh real(kind=wp), intent(inout) :: hTr_layer (:,:) real(kind=wp), intent(in) :: dt real(kind=wp), intent(in), optional :: bc_tr_ext (MAX_BC_GROUPS) External tracer concentration for each boundary group.\nUsed for inflow BCs. If not provided, inflow boundaries\nuse zero tracer concentration. Calls proc~~ml_advect_tracer_unstr~~CallsGraph proc~ml_advect_tracer_unstr ml_advect_tracer_unstr proc~advect_tracer_one_cell advect_tracer_one_cell proc~ml_advect_tracer_unstr->proc~advect_tracer_one_cell Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Interface Interface Type Bound Procedure Type Bound Procedure Unknown Procedure Type Unknown Procedure Type Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a procedure to one which it calls. Dashed \narrows point from an interface to procedures which implement that interface.\nThis could include the module procedures in a generic interface or the\nimplementation in a submodule of an interface in a parent module. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Called by proc~~ml_advect_tracer_unstr~~CalledByGraph proc~ml_advect_tracer_unstr ml_advect_tracer_unstr proc~solver_unstr_step solver_unstr_step proc~solver_unstr_step->proc~ml_advect_tracer_unstr proc~rki_unstr_step rki_unstr_step proc~rki_unstr_step->proc~solver_unstr_step proc~solver_unstr_advance solver_unstr_advance proc~solver_unstr_advance->proc~solver_unstr_step proc~driver_run_coastal_unstr driver_run_coastal_unstr proc~driver_run_coastal_unstr->proc~solver_unstr_advance proc~rki_unstr_advance rki_unstr_advance proc~rki_unstr_advance->proc~solver_unstr_advance proc~driver_run driver_run proc~driver_run->proc~driver_run_coastal_unstr Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Interface Interface Type Bound Procedure Type Bound Procedure Unknown Procedure Type Unknown Procedure Type Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a procedure to one which it calls. Dashed \narrows point from an interface to procedures which implement that interface.\nThis could include the module procedures in a generic interface or the\nimplementation in a submodule of an interface in a parent module. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Variables Type Visibility Attributes Name Initial real(kind=wp), private :: bc_ext_local (MAX_BC_GROUPS) integer, private :: cell integer, private :: k integer, private :: n integer, private :: nz Source Code pure subroutine ml_advect_tracer_unstr ( state , mesh , hTr_layer , dt , bc_tr_ext ) !! First-order upwind horizontal tracer advection on triangles !! using a SHEAR-AWARE consistency-with-continuity (CWC) per-layer !! mass flux. Mirrors what the structured `ml_advect_tracer` does !! with its `0.5*(hu_layer left + right)` face flux, but reshaped !! for the cell-centric triangle pattern. !! !! Per-layer mass flux at an interior edge: !!   v_layer(k) = -(hu_edge_k * n1 + hv_edge_k * n2) * L_e !!     where hu_edge_k = 0.5 * (hu_layer(k, here) + hu_layer(k, nbr)) !!   correction = mass_flux_e - sum_k v_layer(k) !!   f_layer(k) = v_layer(k) + dsig_k * correction !! !! The construction enforces `sum_k f_layer(k) = mass_flux_e` !! exactly, so per-cell tracer mass conservation matches the !! barotropic continuity update. The *distribution* between !! layers reflects the actual per-layer momentum, so a buoyant !! plume (surface seaward / bottom return flow) advects tracer !! in the right direction at every layer. !! !! For depth-uniform velocity (no baroclinic shear) `v_layer(k) = !! dsig_k * sum_k v_layer` and the correction term cancels the !! cell-avg-edge-vs-CU-flux mismatch exactly, so the scheme !! reduces to `f_layer(k) = dsig_k * mass_flux_e`. Uniform !! tracer is preserved bit-perfectly when there is no shear, !! which is the property `test_tracer_uniform_stays_uniform` !! checks (it gates on `beta_S = alpha_T = 0`). !! !! For boundary edges we fall back to `f_layer(k) = dsig_k * !! mass_flux_e` (no neighbour to average against; the BC kernel !! has already prescribed the barotropic mass flux there). !! !! Known limitation: per-cell mass conservation is exact, but !! per-layer hS evolution does not exactly track per-layer !! `dsig_k * H_new` evolution under shear. As a result, the !! scheme can produce small bounded over- or undershoots !! (typically << 1 PSU at sharp baroclinic fronts). The !! `S_min`/`S_max` clamp in `ml_clamp_tracer_unstr` is the !! safety net. The proper fix is an ALE-style remap of hS !! between the per-layer-advected mass and the sigma h after !! distribute -- not landing in this PR. !! !! Boundary handling: !!   - Wall edges: CU flux kernel writes mass_flux_e = 0, so no !!     tracer flux through walls automatically. !!   - Inflow edges (mass_flux_e > 0 at boundary): use bc_tr_ext !!     indexed by the edge's boundary group. !!   - Outflow edges: upwind is interior cell, handled normally. !! !! Cell-centric double-visit pattern preserved: each owned !! triangle writes only into its own `hTr_layer` column. The !! pair of visits on interior edges produces equal-and-opposite !! contributions because `mass_flux_e(e, K) = -mass_flux_e(e', !! N)` across a shared edge and both sides see the same upwind !! tracer value. !! !! Jacobi-vs-Gauss-Seidel note: we snapshot the input `hTr_layer` !! into `state%multilayer%tracer_advect_scratch` on entry and have the !! kernel body read from the scratch for BOTH the own-cell and !! neighbour-cell concentrations, writing only to `hTr_layer`. !! Without this, the enclosing `do concurrent` over owned cells !! produces correct results on GPU (launch-time Jacobi semantics) !! but biased results on CPU, where `do concurrent` collapses to !! a sequential / OMP loop and later cell iterations observe !! already-updated neighbour values. The result is a drift of !! ~3e-6 per step on a uniform tracer that compounds over time. type ( unstr_state_t ), intent ( inout ) :: state type ( mesh_unstr_t ), intent ( in ) :: mesh ! assumed-shape-ok: unstructured outer-shim — ncells varies per mesh partition ! (see CLAUDE.md outer-shim + flat-impl pattern). real ( wp ), intent ( inout ) :: hTr_layer (:, :) real ( wp ), intent ( in ) :: dt real ( wp ), intent ( in ), optional :: bc_tr_ext ( MAX_BC_GROUPS ) !! External tracer concentration for each boundary group. !! Used for inflow BCs. If not provided, inflow boundaries !! use zero tracer concentration. integer :: cell , k , nz , n real ( wp ) :: bc_ext_local ( MAX_BC_GROUPS ) if (. not . state % use_multilayer ) return nz = state % multilayer % nz_ml n = state % ncells_total ! Copy bc_tr_ext to a local array so it can be passed to the ! pure per-cell kernel. Default to zero if not provided. if ( present ( bc_tr_ext )) then bc_ext_local = bc_tr_ext else bc_ext_local = 0.0_wp end if ! Snapshot the input tracer into the scratch buffer so the per-cell ! kernel can read neighbour concentrations from a frozen image and ! write the update into the live buffer without a race. do concurrent ( cell = 1 : n , k = 1 : nz ) state % multilayer % tracer_advect_scratch ( k , cell ) = hTr_layer ( k , cell ) end do do concurrent ( cell = 1 : mesh % ncells_owned ) call advect_tracer_one_cell ( state , mesh , hTr_layer , nz , dt , cell , & bc_ext_local ) end do end subroutine ml_advect_tracer_unstr","tags":"","url":"proc/ml_advect_tracer_unstr.html"},{"title":"ml_apply_surface_tracer_fluxes_unstr – Rakali","text":"public pure subroutine ml_apply_surface_tracer_fluxes_unstr(state, hS_layer, hT_layer) Apply prescribed surface fluxes to the top layer (k=nz) of every\ncell.  Two evaporation modes, mirroring the structured ml_apply_surface_tracer_fluxes : Default (evap_mass_sink = .false.) — virtual salt flux: hT(nz, c) += dt * heat_flux(c) / (rho_0 * cp)    ! thermal\n  hS(nz, c) += dt * (E - P)(c) * S_top             ! virtual salt Mass-sink mode (evap_mass_sink = .true.) — real water removal: net evaporation dh = E*dt is removed COLUMN-PROPORTIONALLY — every\nlayer’s h, hu, hv, hT is scaled by fac = 1 - dh/h_col while hS is\nheld (salt concentrates, T/u/v invariant).  The removal is clamped\nto the water above ML_MIN_DEPTH_UNSTR; the unsatisfied fraction is\ndropped honestly.  Precipitation (E - P < 0) is NOT added here.\nThe barotropic state is refreshed from the scaled layers by the ml_compute_barotropic_momentum_unstr call that follows every\nsurface-flux call in the unstructured RK2 pipeline, so the depth\ndrawdown propagates to height_cv / stage_cv .  This is a\nper-cell kernel writing only its OWN column (no neighbour scatter). evap_latent_cooling (needs evap_mass_sink): the evaporated mass\nadditionally withdraws its latent heat of vaporization from the top\nlayer.  hT(nz) is fac-scaled by the sensible sink FIRST; the latent\nterm is the additive increment applied AFTER that scaling (NOT the\nfull hT(nz) update): hT(nz) += -(rho_water L_v/(rho_0 cp))*dh_eff —\nan ADDITIVE surface cooling, distinct from the sensible heat that\nleaves with the removed mass (no double-count). Layer-index convention: the unstructured solver indexes layers\nbottom-up (k=1 is the bottom sigma layer, k=nz is the surface\nlayer just below the free surface) — see the header of rki_ml_dynamics_unstr .  Surface forcings therefore land at\nk=nz, mirroring the structured side after both adopted the\nsame ROMS convention. Heat flux is in W/m&#94;2, positive into the ocean.  E - P is in\nm/s, positive for net evaporation (concentrates salinity in the\ntop layer).  Neither term changes h_layer – this is pure\ntracer forcing in the ROMS / MOM convention. Mirrors ml_apply_surface_tracer_fluxes on the structured side\n( rki_ml_tracers.F90 ) modulo the axis-order swap (unstructured\nis (nz, ncells_total) ).  Must run on every RK2 stage to\npreserve second-order accuracy in time. Reads the forcing fields directly off state%forcing ; both\narrays are zero-filled at init so the kernel is a bit-exact\nno-op until a setter pushes non-zero values. Only owned-cell tracer columns are written. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( unstr_state_t ), intent(inout) :: state real(kind=wp), intent(inout) :: hS_layer (:,:) real(kind=wp), intent(inout) :: hT_layer (:,:) Calls proc~~ml_apply_surface_tracer_fluxes_unstr~~CallsGraph proc~ml_apply_surface_tracer_fluxes_unstr ml_apply_surface_tracer_fluxes_unstr local local proc~ml_apply_surface_tracer_fluxes_unstr->local Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Interface Interface Type Bound Procedure Type Bound Procedure Unknown Procedure Type Unknown Procedure Type Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a procedure to one which it calls. Dashed \narrows point from an interface to procedures which implement that interface.\nThis could include the module procedures in a generic interface or the\nimplementation in a submodule of an interface in a parent module. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Called by proc~~ml_apply_surface_tracer_fluxes_unstr~~CalledByGraph proc~ml_apply_surface_tracer_fluxes_unstr ml_apply_surface_tracer_fluxes_unstr proc~si_unstr_step_ml si_unstr_step_ml proc~si_unstr_step_ml->proc~ml_apply_surface_tracer_fluxes_unstr proc~solver_unstr_step solver_unstr_step proc~solver_unstr_step->proc~ml_apply_surface_tracer_fluxes_unstr proc~solver_unstr_step_semi_implicit solver_unstr_step_semi_implicit proc~solver_unstr_step->proc~solver_unstr_step_semi_implicit proc~rki_unstr_step rki_unstr_step proc~rki_unstr_step->proc~solver_unstr_step proc~solver_unstr_advance solver_unstr_advance proc~solver_unstr_advance->proc~solver_unstr_step proc~solver_unstr_step_semi_implicit->proc~si_unstr_step_ml proc~driver_run_coastal_unstr driver_run_coastal_unstr proc~driver_run_coastal_unstr->proc~solver_unstr_advance proc~rki_unstr_advance rki_unstr_advance proc~rki_unstr_advance->proc~solver_unstr_advance proc~driver_run driver_run proc~driver_run->proc~driver_run_coastal_unstr Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Interface Interface Type Bound Procedure Type Bound Procedure Unknown Procedure Type Unknown Procedure Type Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a procedure to one which it calls. Dashed \narrows point from an interface to procedures which implement that interface.\nThis could include the module procedures in a generic interface or the\nimplementation in a submodule of an interface in a parent module. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Variables Type Visibility Attributes Name Initial real(kind=wp), private :: S_top integer, private :: cell real(kind=wp), private :: dh_eff real(kind=wp), private :: dh_req real(kind=wp), private :: dt logical, private :: evap_mass_sink real(kind=wp), private :: fac real(kind=wp), private :: h_col real(kind=wp), private :: h_top real(kind=wp), private :: inv_rho0cp integer, private :: k real(kind=wp), private :: kin_q real(kind=wp), private :: latent_gain integer, private :: n integer, private :: nz Source Code pure subroutine ml_apply_surface_tracer_fluxes_unstr ( state , hS_layer , hT_layer ) !! Apply prescribed surface fluxes to the top layer (k=nz) of every !! cell.  Two evaporation modes, mirroring the structured !! `ml_apply_surface_tracer_fluxes`: !! !! **Default (evap_mass_sink = .false.) — virtual salt flux:** !!   hT(nz, c) += dt * heat_flux(c) / (rho_0 * cp)    ! thermal !!   hS(nz, c) += dt * (E - P)(c) * S_top             ! virtual salt !! !! **Mass-sink mode (evap_mass_sink = .true.) — real water removal:** !! net evaporation dh = E*dt is removed COLUMN-PROPORTIONALLY — every !! layer's h, hu, hv, hT is scaled by fac = 1 - dh/h_col while hS is !! held (salt concentrates, T/u/v invariant).  The removal is clamped !! to the water above ML_MIN_DEPTH_UNSTR; the unsatisfied fraction is !! dropped honestly.  Precipitation (E - P < 0) is NOT added here. !! The barotropic state is refreshed from the scaled layers by the !! `ml_compute_barotropic_momentum_unstr` call that follows every !! surface-flux call in the unstructured RK2 pipeline, so the depth !! drawdown propagates to `height_cv`/`stage_cv`.  This is a !! per-cell kernel writing only its OWN column (no neighbour scatter). !! !! **evap_latent_cooling** (needs evap_mass_sink): the evaporated mass !! additionally withdraws its latent heat of vaporization from the top !! layer.  hT(nz) is fac-scaled by the sensible sink FIRST; the latent !! term is the additive increment applied AFTER that scaling (NOT the !! full hT(nz) update): hT(nz) += -(rho_water*L_v/(rho_0*cp))*dh_eff — !! an ADDITIVE surface cooling, distinct from the sensible heat that !! leaves with the removed mass (no double-count). !! !! Layer-index convention: the unstructured solver indexes layers !! bottom-up (k=1 is the bottom sigma layer, k=nz is the surface !! layer just below the free surface) — see the header of !! `rki_ml_dynamics_unstr`.  Surface forcings therefore land at !! k=nz, mirroring the structured side after both adopted the !! same ROMS convention. !! !! Heat flux is in W/m&#94;2, positive into the ocean.  E - P is in !! m/s, positive for net evaporation (concentrates salinity in the !! top layer).  Neither term changes h_layer -- this is pure !! tracer forcing in the ROMS / MOM convention. !! !! Mirrors `ml_apply_surface_tracer_fluxes` on the structured side !! (`rki_ml_tracers.F90`) modulo the axis-order swap (unstructured !! is `(nz, ncells_total)`).  Must run on every RK2 stage to !! preserve second-order accuracy in time. !! !! Reads the forcing fields directly off `state%forcing`; both !! arrays are zero-filled at init so the kernel is a bit-exact !! no-op until a setter pushes non-zero values. !! !! Only owned-cell tracer columns are written. type ( unstr_state_t ), intent ( inout ) :: state ! assumed-shape-ok: unstructured outer-shim — ncells varies per mesh partition; ! caller host-dereferences S/T tracer slots before passing ! (see CLAUDE.md outer-shim + flat-impl pattern). real ( wp ), intent ( inout ) :: hS_layer (:, :) real ( wp ), intent ( inout ) :: hT_layer (:, :) ! assumed-shape-ok: unstructured outer-shim; ncells varies per mesh partition integer :: cell , k , nz , n real ( wp ) :: inv_rho0cp , dt , h_top , h_col , S_top , kin_q real ( wp ) :: dh_req , dh_eff , fac , latent_gain logical :: evap_mass_sink if (. not . state % use_multilayer ) return nz = state % multilayer % nz_ml n = state % ncells_total dt = state % dt inv_rho0cp = 1.0_wp / ( state % multilayer % rho_0 * CP_WATER ) ! Hoist the scalar flags to host locals (avoid per-iteration struct ! deref on device; latent_gain == 0 when off -> bit-identical default). evap_mass_sink = state % multilayer % evap_mass_sink latent_gain = 0.0_wp if ( state % multilayer % evap_latent_cooling ) then latent_gain = RHO_WATER * LATENT_HEAT_VAPORIZATION * inv_rho0cp end if do concurrent ( cell = 1 : n ) & local ( k , h_top , h_col , S_top , kin_q , dh_req , dh_eff , fac ) h_col = 0.0_wp do k = 1 , nz h_col = h_col + state % multilayer % h_layer ( k , cell ) end do if ( h_col > ML_MIN_DEPTH_UNSTR ) then h_top = state % multilayer % h_layer ( nz , cell ) if ( h_top > THIN_LAYER_THRESHOLD ) then ! Thermal (both modes): dt * Q_net / (rho_0 * cp)  [m * K] kin_q = state % forcing % heat_flux ( cell ) * inv_rho0cp hT_layer ( nz , cell ) = hT_layer ( nz , cell ) + dt * kin_q if ( evap_mass_sink ) then ! Column-proportional water-mass sink: hS held; h, hu, hv, ! hT scale by fac so S concentrates while T, u, v stay ! invariant.  Clamp to the water above the dry floor. dh_req = dt * max ( state % forcing % evap_minus_precip ( cell ), 0.0_wp ) dh_eff = min ( dh_req , max ( h_col - ML_MIN_DEPTH_UNSTR , 0.0_wp )) if ( dh_eff > 0.0_wp ) then fac = 1.0_wp - dh_eff / h_col do k = 1 , nz state % multilayer % h_layer ( k , cell ) = state % multilayer % h_layer ( k , cell ) * fac state % multilayer % hu_layer ( k , cell ) = state % multilayer % hu_layer ( k , cell ) * fac state % multilayer % hv_layer ( k , cell ) = state % multilayer % hv_layer ( k , cell ) * fac hT_layer ( k , cell ) = hT_layer ( k , cell ) * fac ! hS_layer held -> concentration rises naturally. end do ! Latent-heat cooling (additive, top layer; not scaled ! by fac).  latent_gain == 0 when off -> bit-identical. hT_layer ( nz , cell ) = hT_layer ( nz , cell ) - latent_gain * dh_eff end if else ! Legacy virtual salt flux (bit-identical default). S_top = hS_layer ( nz , cell ) / h_top hS_layer ( nz , cell ) = hS_layer ( nz , cell ) & + dt * state % forcing % evap_minus_precip ( cell ) * S_top end if end if end if end do end subroutine ml_apply_surface_tracer_fluxes_unstr","tags":"","url":"proc/ml_apply_surface_tracer_fluxes_unstr.html"},{"title":"ml_apply_tracer_bcs_unstr – Rakali","text":"public  subroutine ml_apply_tracer_bcs_unstr(state, mesh, hTr_layer, bc_tr_ext) Apply tracer boundary conditions for inflow edges. For boundary edges where mass is flowing into the domain (inflow),\nthis routine sets the tracer concentration to bc_tr_ext. The value\nis written into tracer_advect_scratch so that ml_advect_tracer_unstr sees the correct upwind value at inflow boundaries. Must be called AFTER ml_advect_tracer_unstr has snapshotted hTr_layer into tracer_advect_scratch , but BEFORE the per-cell\nadvection loop runs. Since ml_advect_tracer_unstr does both in\nsequence, this routine should be called between the snapshot and\nthe advection, or the advection kernel needs to be modified to\ncall this internally. Alternative approach (implemented here): For each boundary edge\nwith inflow (mass_flux_e > 0), we don’t need to modify the scratch\n— instead we can post-process the advection to add the missing\nboundary contribution. But this is tricky with the current kernel. Current implementation: mark boundary edges in state and let the\nadvection kernel check bc_tr_ext directly. This requires modifying advect_tracer_one_cell to accept bc_tr_ext and check for boundary\ninflow. For now, this routine is a placeholder that documents the interface.\nThe actual BC application is integrated into the advection kernel. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( unstr_state_t ), intent(inout) :: state type( mesh_unstr_t ), intent(in) :: mesh real(kind=wp), intent(inout) :: hTr_layer (:,:) real(kind=wp), intent(in) :: bc_tr_ext (MAX_BC_GROUPS) Source Code subroutine ml_apply_tracer_bcs_unstr ( state , mesh , hTr_layer , bc_tr_ext ) !! Apply tracer boundary conditions for inflow edges. !! !! For boundary edges where mass is flowing into the domain (inflow), !! this routine sets the tracer concentration to bc_tr_ext. The value !! is written into `tracer_advect_scratch` so that `ml_advect_tracer_unstr` !! sees the correct upwind value at inflow boundaries. !! !! Must be called AFTER `ml_advect_tracer_unstr` has snapshotted !! `hTr_layer` into `tracer_advect_scratch`, but BEFORE the per-cell !! advection loop runs. Since `ml_advect_tracer_unstr` does both in !! sequence, this routine should be called between the snapshot and !! the advection, or the advection kernel needs to be modified to !! call this internally. !! !! Alternative approach (implemented here): For each boundary edge !! with inflow (mass_flux_e > 0), we don't need to modify the scratch !! — instead we can post-process the advection to add the missing !! boundary contribution. But this is tricky with the current kernel. !! !! Current implementation: mark boundary edges in state and let the !! advection kernel check `bc_tr_ext` directly. This requires modifying !! `advect_tracer_one_cell` to accept bc_tr_ext and check for boundary !! inflow. !! !! For now, this routine is a placeholder that documents the interface. !! The actual BC application is integrated into the advection kernel. type ( unstr_state_t ), intent ( inout ) :: state type ( mesh_unstr_t ), intent ( in ) :: mesh real ( wp ), intent ( inout ) :: hTr_layer (:, :) real ( wp ), intent ( in ) :: bc_tr_ext ( MAX_BC_GROUPS ) ! Placeholder — BCs are handled inline in the advection kernel ! by checking mesh%neighbours(e, cell) < 0 and state%barotropic%mass_flux_e > 0 ! for inflow boundaries. ! ! This routine exists so the solver can call a uniform interface. ! Future: implement open-boundary handling here if needed. end subroutine ml_apply_tracer_bcs_unstr","tags":"","url":"proc/ml_apply_tracer_bcs_unstr.html"},{"title":"ml_clamp_tracer_unstr – Rakali","text":"public  subroutine ml_clamp_tracer_unstr(state, hTr_layer, tr_min, tr_max) Enforce physical tracer bounds [tr_min, tr_max] conservatively\nwithin each column.  Two-pass column-conservative limiter\n(mirror of the structured ml_clamp_tracer ): Pass 1 — clip violators, accumulate excess_above (above\n           tr_max, destroyed), deficit_below (below tr_min,\n           created), and per-cell spare capacity in non-\n           saturated layers. Pass 2 — redistribute the net imbalance into available\n           capacity within the same column, proportional to\n           each non-saturated layer’s room-to-fill (or room-\n           to-empty for net < 0). Per-cell tracer total preserved exactly when column-mean is in\nbounds.  Critical for ZSTAR_FULL deep-shallow scenarios where ml_distribute_with_remap_full ’s dz_old_col = remap_dz_old *\nscale_old rescaling inflates per-layer S as a numerical\nartefact; the previous hard clip would destroy real tracer\nthat the conservative remap had just preserved. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( unstr_state_t ), intent(inout) :: state real(kind=wp), intent(inout), contiguous :: hTr_layer (:,:) real(kind=wp), intent(in) :: tr_min real(kind=wp), intent(in) :: tr_max Calls proc~~ml_clamp_tracer_unstr~~CallsGraph proc~ml_clamp_tracer_unstr ml_clamp_tracer_unstr proc~ml_clamp_tracer_unstr_impl ml_clamp_tracer_unstr_impl proc~ml_clamp_tracer_unstr->proc~ml_clamp_tracer_unstr_impl local local proc~ml_clamp_tracer_unstr_impl->local Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Interface Interface Type Bound Procedure Type Bound Procedure Unknown Procedure Type Unknown Procedure Type Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a procedure to one which it calls. Dashed \narrows point from an interface to procedures which implement that interface.\nThis could include the module procedures in a generic interface or the\nimplementation in a submodule of an interface in a parent module. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Called by proc~~ml_clamp_tracer_unstr~~CalledByGraph proc~ml_clamp_tracer_unstr ml_clamp_tracer_unstr proc~si_unstr_step_ml si_unstr_step_ml proc~si_unstr_step_ml->proc~ml_clamp_tracer_unstr proc~solver_unstr_step solver_unstr_step proc~solver_unstr_step->proc~ml_clamp_tracer_unstr proc~solver_unstr_step_semi_implicit solver_unstr_step_semi_implicit proc~solver_unstr_step->proc~solver_unstr_step_semi_implicit proc~rki_unstr_step rki_unstr_step proc~rki_unstr_step->proc~solver_unstr_step proc~solver_unstr_advance solver_unstr_advance proc~solver_unstr_advance->proc~solver_unstr_step proc~solver_unstr_step_semi_implicit->proc~si_unstr_step_ml proc~driver_run_coastal_unstr driver_run_coastal_unstr proc~driver_run_coastal_unstr->proc~solver_unstr_advance proc~rki_unstr_advance rki_unstr_advance proc~rki_unstr_advance->proc~solver_unstr_advance proc~driver_run driver_run proc~driver_run->proc~driver_run_coastal_unstr Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Interface Interface Type Bound Procedure Type Bound Procedure Unknown Procedure Type Unknown Procedure Type Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a procedure to one which it calls. Dashed \narrows point from an interface to procedures which implement that interface.\nThis could include the module procedures in a generic interface or the\nimplementation in a submodule of an interface in a parent module. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Source Code subroutine ml_clamp_tracer_unstr ( state , hTr_layer , tr_min , tr_max ) !! Enforce physical tracer bounds [tr_min, tr_max] conservatively !! within each column.  Two-pass column-conservative limiter !! (mirror of the structured `ml_clamp_tracer`): !! !!   Pass 1 — clip violators, accumulate excess_above (above !!            tr_max, destroyed), deficit_below (below tr_min, !!            created), and per-cell spare capacity in non- !!            saturated layers. !! !!   Pass 2 — redistribute the net imbalance into available !!            capacity within the same column, proportional to !!            each non-saturated layer's room-to-fill (or room- !!            to-empty for net < 0). !! !! Per-cell tracer total preserved exactly when column-mean is in !! bounds.  Critical for ZSTAR_FULL deep-shallow scenarios where !! `ml_distribute_with_remap_full`'s `dz_old_col = remap_dz_old * !! scale_old` rescaling inflates per-layer S as a numerical !! artefact; the previous hard clip would destroy real tracer !! that the conservative remap had just preserved. type ( unstr_state_t ), intent ( inout ) :: state real ( wp ), intent ( inout ), contiguous :: hTr_layer (:, :) real ( wp ), intent ( in ) :: tr_min , tr_max if (. not . state % use_multilayer ) return call ml_clamp_tracer_unstr_impl ( state % multilayer % nz_ml , state % ncells_total , & state % multilayer % h_layer , hTr_layer , tr_min , tr_max ) end subroutine ml_clamp_tracer_unstr","tags":"","url":"proc/ml_clamp_tracer_unstr.html"},{"title":"ml_distribute_tracer_unstr – Rakali","text":"public pure subroutine ml_distribute_tracer_unstr(state, hTr_layer) Preserve tracer concentration across a barotropic depth change. Reads h_layer (pre-update, still carries dsig_k * H_old ),\ncomputes Tr_k = hTr_k / h_layer_k , and writes\n  hTr_k := (dsig_k * H_new) * Tr_k\nso that when ml_distribute_barotropic_unstr overwrites h_layer with the new sigma thicknesses the concentration\nstays fixed. Must be called BEFORE ml_distribute_barotropic_unstr each\nRK stage, once per tracer. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( unstr_state_t ), intent(inout) :: state real(kind=wp), intent(inout) :: hTr_layer (:,:) Calls proc~~ml_distribute_tracer_unstr~~CallsGraph proc~ml_distribute_tracer_unstr ml_distribute_tracer_unstr local local proc~ml_distribute_tracer_unstr->local Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Interface Interface Type Bound Procedure Type Bound Procedure Unknown Procedure Type Unknown Procedure Type Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a procedure to one which it calls. Dashed \narrows point from an interface to procedures which implement that interface.\nThis could include the module procedures in a generic interface or the\nimplementation in a submodule of an interface in a parent module. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Variables Type Visibility Attributes Name Initial integer, private :: cell real(kind=wp), private :: h_k_new real(kind=wp), private :: h_k_old real(kind=wp), private :: h_val integer, private :: k integer, private :: n integer, private :: nz real(kind=wp), private :: tr_k Source Code pure subroutine ml_distribute_tracer_unstr ( state , hTr_layer ) !! Preserve tracer concentration across a barotropic depth change. !! !! Reads `h_layer` (pre-update, still carries `dsig_k * H_old`), !! computes `Tr_k = hTr_k / h_layer_k`, and writes !!   hTr_k := (dsig_k * H_new) * Tr_k !! so that when `ml_distribute_barotropic_unstr` overwrites !! `h_layer` with the new sigma thicknesses the concentration !! stays fixed. !! !! Must be called BEFORE `ml_distribute_barotropic_unstr` each !! RK stage, once per tracer. type ( unstr_state_t ), intent ( inout ) :: state ! assumed-shape-ok: unstructured outer-shim — ncells varies per mesh partition ! (see CLAUDE.md outer-shim + flat-impl pattern). real ( wp ), intent ( inout ) :: hTr_layer (:, :) integer :: cell , k , nz , n real ( wp ) :: h_val , h_k_old , h_k_new , tr_k if (. not . state % use_multilayer ) return nz = state % multilayer % nz_ml n = state % ncells_total do concurrent ( cell = 1 : n ) local ( k , h_val , h_k_old , h_k_new , tr_k ) h_val = state % barotropic % stage_cv ( cell ) - state % barotropic % bed_cv ( cell ) if ( h_val < 0.0_wp ) h_val = 0.0_wp do k = 1 , nz h_k_old = state % multilayer % h_layer ( k , cell ) h_k_new = state % multilayer % dsig_ml ( k ) * h_val if ( h_k_old > DRY_TOLERANCE ) then tr_k = hTr_layer ( k , cell ) / h_k_old else tr_k = 0.0_wp end if hTr_layer ( k , cell ) = h_k_new * tr_k end do end do end subroutine ml_distribute_tracer_unstr","tags":"","url":"proc/ml_distribute_tracer_unstr.html"},{"title":"ml_horizontal_diffusion_tracer_unstr – Rakali","text":"public  subroutine ml_horizontal_diffusion_tracer_unstr(state, mesh, hTr_layer, kappa, dt) Horizontal Laplacian diffusion of a scalar tracer on triangles\nin flux form, conservative per-edge under non-uniform layer\nthicknesses. Per edge the diffusive flux is\n  flux_edge = h_face * kappa_eff * (Tr_nbr - Tr_self) * L_e / d_cn\nwhere h_face = 0.5 * (h_self + h_nbr) is symmetric across the\nedge.  Accumulated as d(hTr)/dt = (1/A) * sum_e flux_edge , so\nwhat cell A gains from edge A-B equals what cell B loses\nthrough the shared edge — mass conservation holds even when\nthe per-cell layer thicknesses differ across the edge. History: the previous formulation computed lap_tr = (1/A) *\nsum_e (Tr_nbr - Tr_self) * L_e / d_cn (per-edge concentration\ngradient with no h_face weighting) and updated hTr_new =\nh_self * (Tr_self + kappa·dt·lap_tr) .  Each cell used its\nOWN h on the RHS, so adjacent cells with different h_layer(k) (zsigma / zstar at bathymetric transitions) saw\ndifferent mass changes through the same shared edge, leaking\ntotal mass.  Currently dormant in production runs\n( hdiff_kappa = 0 in the LE / Sydney Harbour configs) but\nwould bite the moment any user enables it. Boundary edges (surrogate_neighbours == self) and dry-neighbour\nedges contribute zero (closed face), preserving the symmetric\nper-face conservation property. Jacobi snapshot: hTr_pre freezes the input so CPU-sequential\nand GPU-parallel builds give bit-identical results. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( unstr_state_t ), intent(inout) :: state type( mesh_unstr_t ), intent(in) :: mesh real(kind=wp), intent(inout), contiguous :: hTr_layer (:,:) real(kind=wp), intent(in) :: kappa real(kind=wp), intent(in) :: dt Calls proc~~ml_horizontal_diffusion_tracer_unstr~~CallsGraph proc~ml_horizontal_diffusion_tracer_unstr ml_horizontal_diffusion_tracer_unstr proc~ml_horizontal_diffusion_tracer_unstr_impl ml_horizontal_diffusion_tracer_unstr_impl proc~ml_horizontal_diffusion_tracer_unstr->proc~ml_horizontal_diffusion_tracer_unstr_impl local local proc~ml_horizontal_diffusion_tracer_unstr_impl->local Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Interface Interface Type Bound Procedure Type Bound Procedure Unknown Procedure Type Unknown Procedure Type Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a procedure to one which it calls. Dashed \narrows point from an interface to procedures which implement that interface.\nThis could include the module procedures in a generic interface or the\nimplementation in a submodule of an interface in a parent module. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Called by proc~~ml_horizontal_diffusion_tracer_unstr~~CalledByGraph proc~ml_horizontal_diffusion_tracer_unstr ml_horizontal_diffusion_tracer_unstr proc~solver_unstr_step solver_unstr_step proc~solver_unstr_step->proc~ml_horizontal_diffusion_tracer_unstr proc~rki_unstr_step rki_unstr_step proc~rki_unstr_step->proc~solver_unstr_step proc~solver_unstr_advance solver_unstr_advance proc~solver_unstr_advance->proc~solver_unstr_step proc~driver_run_coastal_unstr driver_run_coastal_unstr proc~driver_run_coastal_unstr->proc~solver_unstr_advance proc~rki_unstr_advance rki_unstr_advance proc~rki_unstr_advance->proc~solver_unstr_advance proc~driver_run driver_run proc~driver_run->proc~driver_run_coastal_unstr Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Interface Interface Type Bound Procedure Type Bound Procedure Unknown Procedure Type Unknown Procedure Type Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a procedure to one which it calls. Dashed \narrows point from an interface to procedures which implement that interface.\nThis could include the module procedures in a generic interface or the\nimplementation in a submodule of an interface in a parent module. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Source Code subroutine ml_horizontal_diffusion_tracer_unstr ( state , mesh , hTr_layer , kappa , dt ) !! Horizontal Laplacian diffusion of a scalar tracer on triangles !! in flux form, conservative per-edge under non-uniform layer !! thicknesses. !! !! Per edge the diffusive flux is !!   flux_edge = h_face * kappa_eff * (Tr_nbr - Tr_self) * L_e / d_cn !! where `h_face = 0.5 * (h_self + h_nbr)` is symmetric across the !! edge.  Accumulated as `d(hTr)/dt = (1/A) * sum_e flux_edge`, so !! what cell A gains from edge A-B equals what cell B loses !! through the shared edge — mass conservation holds even when !! the per-cell layer thicknesses differ across the edge. !! !! History: the previous formulation computed `lap_tr = (1/A) * !! sum_e (Tr_nbr - Tr_self) * L_e / d_cn` (per-edge concentration !! gradient with no `h_face` weighting) and updated `hTr_new = !! h_self * (Tr_self + kappa·dt·lap_tr)`.  Each cell used its !! OWN `h` on the RHS, so adjacent cells with different !! `h_layer(k)` (zsigma / zstar at bathymetric transitions) saw !! different mass changes through the same shared edge, leaking !! total mass.  Currently dormant in production runs !! (`hdiff_kappa = 0` in the LE / Sydney Harbour configs) but !! would bite the moment any user enables it. !! !! Boundary edges (surrogate_neighbours == self) and dry-neighbour !! edges contribute zero (closed face), preserving the symmetric !! per-face conservation property. !! !! Jacobi snapshot: `hTr_pre` freezes the input so CPU-sequential !! and GPU-parallel builds give bit-identical results. type ( unstr_state_t ), intent ( inout ) :: state type ( mesh_unstr_t ), intent ( in ) :: mesh real ( wp ), intent ( inout ), contiguous :: hTr_layer (:, :) real ( wp ), intent ( in ) :: kappa real ( wp ), intent ( in ) :: dt if ( kappa <= 0.0_wp ) return if (. not . state % use_multilayer ) return call ml_horizontal_diffusion_tracer_unstr_impl ( & state % multilayer % nz_ml , state % ncells_total , state % ncells_owned , & state % barotropic % height_cv , state % multilayer % h_layer , & state % multilayer % ml_hdiff_hTr_pre , mesh % area , mesh % radius , & mesh % centroid_x , mesh % centroid_y , mesh % edge_len , & mesh % surrogate_neighbours , hTr_layer , kappa , dt ) end subroutine ml_horizontal_diffusion_tracer_unstr","tags":"","url":"proc/ml_horizontal_diffusion_tracer_unstr.html"},{"title":"ml_init_tracer_stratified_unstr – Rakali","text":"public pure subroutine ml_init_tracer_stratified_unstr(state, hTr_layer, tr_bottom, tr_surface) Seed a tracer with a linear vertical profile: tr_bottom at k=1\n(bed) to tr_surface at k=nz (surface), per the bottom-up layer\nconvention.  The per-column entry point anticipated by ml_init_tracer_unstr .  The profile is computed inline from the\ntwo scalars (no host-side per-layer array) so the seed runs\ncorrectly on the device after enter_data .  Caller guarantees\nnz > 1.\n  hTr_k(cell) = h_layer_k(cell) * [tr_bottom + dtr*(k - 1)] Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( unstr_state_t ), intent(inout) :: state real(kind=wp), intent(inout) :: hTr_layer (:,:) real(kind=wp), intent(in) :: tr_bottom real(kind=wp), intent(in) :: tr_surface Called by proc~~ml_init_tracer_stratified_unstr~~CalledByGraph proc~ml_init_tracer_stratified_unstr ml_init_tracer_stratified_unstr proc~ml_seed_tracer_unstr ml_seed_tracer_unstr proc~ml_seed_tracer_unstr->proc~ml_init_tracer_stratified_unstr proc~ml_seed_standard_tracers_unstr ml_seed_standard_tracers_unstr proc~ml_seed_standard_tracers_unstr->proc~ml_seed_tracer_unstr proc~solver_unstr_init_multilayer solver_unstr_init_multilayer proc~solver_unstr_init_multilayer->proc~ml_seed_standard_tracers_unstr proc~driver_run_coastal_unstr driver_run_coastal_unstr proc~driver_run_coastal_unstr->proc~solver_unstr_init_multilayer proc~rki_unstr_finalize rki_unstr_finalize proc~rki_unstr_finalize->proc~solver_unstr_init_multilayer proc~driver_run driver_run proc~driver_run->proc~driver_run_coastal_unstr Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Interface Interface Type Bound Procedure Type Bound Procedure Unknown Procedure Type Unknown Procedure Type Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a procedure to one which it calls. Dashed \narrows point from an interface to procedures which implement that interface.\nThis could include the module procedures in a generic interface or the\nimplementation in a submodule of an interface in a parent module. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Variables Type Visibility Attributes Name Initial integer, private :: cell real(kind=wp), private :: dtr integer, private :: k integer, private :: n integer, private :: nz Source Code pure subroutine ml_init_tracer_stratified_unstr ( state , hTr_layer , tr_bottom , tr_surface ) !! Seed a tracer with a linear vertical profile: `tr_bottom` at k=1 !! (bed) to `tr_surface` at k=nz (surface), per the bottom-up layer !! convention.  The per-column entry point anticipated by !! `ml_init_tracer_unstr`.  The profile is computed inline from the !! two scalars (no host-side per-layer array) so the seed runs !! correctly on the device after `enter_data`.  Caller guarantees !! nz > 1. !!   hTr_k(cell) = h_layer_k(cell) * [tr_bottom + dtr*(k - 1)] type ( unstr_state_t ), intent ( inout ) :: state real ( wp ), intent ( inout ) :: hTr_layer (:, :) ! assumed-shape-ok: unstructured outer-shim; ncells varies per mesh partition real ( wp ), intent ( in ) :: tr_bottom , tr_surface integer :: cell , k , nz , n real ( wp ) :: dtr if (. not . state % use_multilayer ) return nz = state % multilayer % nz_ml n = state % ncells_total dtr = ( tr_surface - tr_bottom ) / real ( nz - 1 , wp ) do concurrent ( cell = 1 : n , k = 1 : nz ) hTr_layer ( k , cell ) = state % multilayer % h_layer ( k , cell ) & * ( tr_bottom + dtr * real ( k - 1 , wp )) end do end subroutine ml_init_tracer_stratified_unstr","tags":"","url":"proc/ml_init_tracer_stratified_unstr.html"},{"title":"ml_init_tracer_unstr – Rakali","text":"public pure subroutine ml_init_tracer_unstr(state, hTr_layer, tr_init) Initialise a tracer field from a uniform scalar value:\n  hTr_k(cell) = h_layer_k(cell) * tr_init\nCalled once per tracer after ml_init_layers_unstr has\npopulated h_layer . Future phases will accept per-column\nprofiles via a separate entry point. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( unstr_state_t ), intent(inout) :: state real(kind=wp), intent(inout) :: hTr_layer (:,:) real(kind=wp), intent(in) :: tr_init Called by proc~~ml_init_tracer_unstr~~CalledByGraph proc~ml_init_tracer_unstr ml_init_tracer_unstr proc~ml_seed_tracer_unstr ml_seed_tracer_unstr proc~ml_seed_tracer_unstr->proc~ml_init_tracer_unstr proc~solver_unstr_init_multilayer solver_unstr_init_multilayer proc~solver_unstr_init_multilayer->proc~ml_init_tracer_unstr proc~ml_seed_standard_tracers_unstr ml_seed_standard_tracers_unstr proc~solver_unstr_init_multilayer->proc~ml_seed_standard_tracers_unstr proc~driver_run_coastal_unstr driver_run_coastal_unstr proc~driver_run_coastal_unstr->proc~solver_unstr_init_multilayer proc~ml_seed_standard_tracers_unstr->proc~ml_seed_tracer_unstr proc~rki_unstr_finalize rki_unstr_finalize proc~rki_unstr_finalize->proc~solver_unstr_init_multilayer proc~driver_run driver_run proc~driver_run->proc~driver_run_coastal_unstr Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Interface Interface Type Bound Procedure Type Bound Procedure Unknown Procedure Type Unknown Procedure Type Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a procedure to one which it calls. Dashed \narrows point from an interface to procedures which implement that interface.\nThis could include the module procedures in a generic interface or the\nimplementation in a submodule of an interface in a parent module. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Variables Type Visibility Attributes Name Initial integer, private :: cell integer, private :: k integer, private :: n integer, private :: nz Source Code pure subroutine ml_init_tracer_unstr ( state , hTr_layer , tr_init ) !! Initialise a tracer field from a uniform scalar value: !!   hTr_k(cell) = h_layer_k(cell) * tr_init !! Called once per tracer after `ml_init_layers_unstr` has !! populated `h_layer`. Future phases will accept per-column !! profiles via a separate entry point. type ( unstr_state_t ), intent ( inout ) :: state ! assumed-shape-ok: unstructured outer-shim — ncells varies per mesh partition; ! called once at init (see CLAUDE.md outer-shim + flat-impl pattern). real ( wp ), intent ( inout ) :: hTr_layer (:, :) real ( wp ), intent ( in ) :: tr_init integer :: cell , k , nz , n if (. not . state % use_multilayer ) return nz = state % multilayer % nz_ml n = state % ncells_total do concurrent ( cell = 1 : n , k = 1 : nz ) hTr_layer ( k , cell ) = state % multilayer % h_layer ( k , cell ) * tr_init end do end subroutine ml_init_tracer_unstr","tags":"","url":"proc/ml_init_tracer_unstr.html"},{"title":"ml_rk2_average_tracer_unstr – Rakali","text":"public pure subroutine ml_rk2_average_tracer_unstr(state, hTr_layer, hTr_layer0) RK2 blend for tracer fields: hTr := 0.5*(hTr + hTr&#94;n). This element-wise blend is mass-conservative\n(sum_k 0.5 (hTr_layer0+hTr_layer) = 0.5 (M_0 + M_after_stage2),\nthe SSP-RK2 of advected column mass) so the tracer total\ntracks the barotropic continuity exactly.  It is geometrically\nconsistent when the layer layout doesn’t change across the\nstep, which is the steady-H regime.  Layout-mismatch issues\nat zsigma transition zones are addressed by initialising h_layer directly from vcoord ( ml_init_layers_unstr ) so\nstep-to-step layouts already match. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( unstr_state_t ), intent(inout) :: state real(kind=wp), intent(inout) :: hTr_layer (:,:) real(kind=wp), intent(in) :: hTr_layer0 (:,:) Called by proc~~ml_rk2_average_tracer_unstr~~CalledByGraph proc~ml_rk2_average_tracer_unstr ml_rk2_average_tracer_unstr proc~solver_unstr_step solver_unstr_step proc~solver_unstr_step->proc~ml_rk2_average_tracer_unstr proc~rki_unstr_step rki_unstr_step proc~rki_unstr_step->proc~solver_unstr_step proc~solver_unstr_advance solver_unstr_advance proc~solver_unstr_advance->proc~solver_unstr_step proc~driver_run_coastal_unstr driver_run_coastal_unstr proc~driver_run_coastal_unstr->proc~solver_unstr_advance proc~rki_unstr_advance rki_unstr_advance proc~rki_unstr_advance->proc~solver_unstr_advance proc~driver_run driver_run proc~driver_run->proc~driver_run_coastal_unstr Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Interface Interface Type Bound Procedure Type Bound Procedure Unknown Procedure Type Unknown Procedure Type Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a procedure to one which it calls. Dashed \narrows point from an interface to procedures which implement that interface.\nThis could include the module procedures in a generic interface or the\nimplementation in a submodule of an interface in a parent module. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Variables Type Visibility Attributes Name Initial integer, private :: cell integer, private :: k integer, private :: n integer, private :: nz Source Code pure subroutine ml_rk2_average_tracer_unstr ( state , hTr_layer , hTr_layer0 ) !! RK2 blend for tracer fields: hTr := 0.5*(hTr + hTr&#94;n). !! !! This element-wise blend is mass-conservative !! (sum_k 0.5*(hTr_layer0+hTr_layer) = 0.5*(M_0 + M_after_stage2), !! the SSP-RK2 of advected column mass) so the tracer total !! tracks the barotropic continuity exactly.  It is geometrically !! consistent when the layer layout doesn't change across the !! step, which is the steady-H regime.  Layout-mismatch issues !! at zsigma transition zones are addressed by initialising !! `h_layer` directly from vcoord (`ml_init_layers_unstr`) so !! step-to-step layouts already match. type ( unstr_state_t ), intent ( inout ) :: state ! assumed-shape-ok: unstructured outer-shim — ncells varies per mesh partition ! (see CLAUDE.md outer-shim + flat-impl pattern). real ( wp ), intent ( inout ) :: hTr_layer (:, :) real ( wp ), intent ( in ) :: hTr_layer0 (:, :) ! assumed-shape-ok: unstructured outer-shim; ncells varies per mesh partition integer :: cell , k , nz , n if (. not . state % use_multilayer ) return nz = state % multilayer % nz_ml n = state % ncells_total do concurrent ( cell = 1 : n , k = 1 : nz ) hTr_layer ( k , cell ) = 0.5_wp * ( hTr_layer ( k , cell ) & + hTr_layer0 ( k , cell )) end do end subroutine ml_rk2_average_tracer_unstr","tags":"","url":"proc/ml_rk2_average_tracer_unstr.html"},{"title":"ml_save_tracer_unstr – Rakali","text":"public pure subroutine ml_save_tracer_unstr(state, hTr_layer, hTr_layer0) Snapshot a tracer field at step start for SSP-RK2 averaging. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( unstr_state_t ), intent(inout) :: state real(kind=wp), intent(in) :: hTr_layer (:,:) real(kind=wp), in