on in a submodule of an interface in a parent module. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Variables Type Visibility Attributes Name Initial integer, private :: iters Source Code subroutine solver_unstr_step_semi_implicit ( state , mesh , dt ) !! One semi-implicit (Casulli) free-surface step on the triangulation. !! Hands the conserved variables to `si_unstr_step`, which owns the frozen-Hf predictor -> !! TPFA Helmholtz eta-solve -> edge back-substitution -> A-grid cell !! momentum reconstruction, plus the time/step bookkeeping.  Open-edge !! Dirichlet coupling (tidal / clamped) is read inside the step from !! `state%bc_type` / `state%bc_eta_ext` (the coefficient-space route); !! wall edges get Hf=0 (no-flux Neumann).  The caller sets `bc_eta_ext` !! to the prescribed surface (tests evaluate the tide at the !! theta-weighted time).  The step ends with barotropic stage_cv / !! xmom_cv / ymom_cv / height_cv valid — downstream unchanged. type ( unstr_state_t ), intent ( inout ) :: state type ( mesh_unstr_t ), intent ( inout ) :: mesh real ( wp ), intent ( in ) :: dt integer :: iters ! The ML/2D split MUST match state_unstr_enable_si's slot choice: an ! nz_ml = 1 \"multilayer\" state gets the 2D si slot (the column solve ! needs >= 2 layers), so dispatching on use_multilayer alone would ! run the ML step against unallocated column slots. if ( state % use_multilayer . and . state % multilayer % nz_ml > 1 ) then ! P3.2: the 3D multilayer column-coupled SI step (per-column ! implicit vertical viscosity + Hhat/Gbar coupling into the SAME ! TPFA eta-solve + dsig-proportional registry tracer advection). call si_unstr_step_ml ( state , mesh , dt , iters ) else call si_unstr_step ( state , mesh , dt , iters ) end if end subroutine solver_unstr_step_semi_implicit","tags":"","url":"proc/solver_unstr_step_semi_implicit.html"},{"title":"ml_horizontal_viscosity_smag_unstr – Rakali","text":"public pure subroutine ml_horizontal_viscosity_smag_unstr(state, mesh, dt) Smagorinsky (1963) horizontal viscosity for layer momentum on triangles.\nVelocity gradients via Green-Gauss; nu_h = (Cs&#94;2 A) |S|, stability-limited;\nFVM Laplacian. Jacobi snapshot on hu/hv for CPU/GPU consistency. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( unstr_state_t ), intent(inout) :: state type( mesh_unstr_t ), intent(in) :: mesh real(kind=wp), intent(in) :: dt Calls proc~~ml_horizontal_viscosity_smag_unstr~~CallsGraph proc~ml_horizontal_viscosity_smag_unstr ml_horizontal_viscosity_smag_unstr local local proc~ml_horizontal_viscosity_smag_unstr->local Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Interface Interface Type Bound Procedure Type Bound Procedure Unknown Procedure Type Unknown Procedure Type Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a procedure to one which it calls. Dashed \narrows point from an interface to procedures which implement that interface.\nThis could include the module procedures in a generic interface or the\nimplementation in a submodule of an interface in a parent module. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Called by proc~~ml_horizontal_viscosity_smag_unstr~~CalledByGraph proc~ml_horizontal_viscosity_smag_unstr ml_horizontal_viscosity_smag_unstr proc~si_unstr_step_ml si_unstr_step_ml proc~si_unstr_step_ml->proc~ml_horizontal_viscosity_smag_unstr proc~solver_unstr_step solver_unstr_step proc~solver_unstr_step->proc~ml_horizontal_viscosity_smag_unstr proc~solver_unstr_step_semi_implicit solver_unstr_step_semi_implicit proc~solver_unstr_step->proc~solver_unstr_step_semi_implicit proc~rki_unstr_step rki_unstr_step proc~rki_unstr_step->proc~solver_unstr_step proc~solver_unstr_advance solver_unstr_advance proc~solver_unstr_advance->proc~solver_unstr_step proc~solver_unstr_step_semi_implicit->proc~si_unstr_step_ml proc~driver_run_coastal_unstr driver_run_coastal_unstr proc~driver_run_coastal_unstr->proc~solver_unstr_advance proc~rki_unstr_advance rki_unstr_advance proc~rki_unstr_advance->proc~solver_unstr_advance proc~driver_run driver_run proc~driver_run->proc~driver_run_coastal_unstr Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Interface Interface Type Bound Procedure Type Bound Procedure Unknown Procedure Type Unknown Procedure Type Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a procedure to one which it calls. Dashed \narrows point from an interface to procedures which implement that interface.\nThis could include the module procedures in a generic interface or the\nimplementation in a submodule of an interface in a parent module. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Variables Type Visibility Attributes Name Initial integer, private :: cell real(kind=wp), private :: cs real(kind=wp), private :: cs2_area real(kind=wp), private :: d_cn real(kind=wp), private :: dudx real(kind=wp), private :: dudy real(kind=wp), private :: dvdx real(kind=wp), private :: dvdy real(kind=wp), private :: dx_cn real(kind=wp), private :: dy_cn integer, private :: e real(kind=wp), private :: h_k real(kind=wp), private :: inv_area integer, private :: k real(kind=wp), private :: lap_u real(kind=wp), private :: lap_v integer, private :: n integer, private :: nbr real(kind=wp), private :: nu_h real(kind=wp), private :: nu_max_cell integer, private :: nz real(kind=wp), private :: strain real(kind=wp), private :: u_c real(kind=wp), private :: u_edge real(kind=wp), private :: u_nbr real(kind=wp), private :: v_c real(kind=wp), private :: v_edge real(kind=wp), private :: v_nbr Source Code pure subroutine ml_horizontal_viscosity_smag_unstr ( state , mesh , dt ) !! Smagorinsky (1963) horizontal viscosity for layer momentum on triangles. !! Velocity gradients via Green-Gauss; nu_h = (Cs&#94;2*A)*|S|, stability-limited; !! FVM Laplacian. Jacobi snapshot on hu/hv for CPU/GPU consistency. type ( unstr_state_t ), intent ( inout ) :: state type ( mesh_unstr_t ), intent ( in ) :: mesh real ( wp ), intent ( in ) :: dt integer :: cell , k , e , nbr , nz , n real ( wp ) :: cs , cs2_area , inv_area , nu_max_cell real ( wp ) :: h_k , u_c , v_c , u_nbr , v_nbr , u_edge , v_edge real ( wp ) :: dudx , dvdy , dudy , dvdx , strain , nu_h real ( wp ) :: dx_cn , dy_cn , d_cn , lap_u , lap_v cs = state % multilayer % smagorinsky_cs if ( cs <= 0.0_wp ) return if (. not . state % use_multilayer ) return nz = state % multilayer % nz_ml n = state % ncells_owned ! Jacobi snapshots live on the state struct (persistent, GPU-resident). associate ( hu_pre => state % multilayer % ml_horvisc_hu_pre , & hv_pre => state % multilayer % ml_horvisc_hv_pre ) do concurrent ( k = 1 : nz , cell = 1 : mesh % ncells_total ) hu_pre ( k , cell ) = state % multilayer % hu_layer ( k , cell ) hv_pre ( k , cell ) = state % multilayer % hv_layer ( k , cell ) end do do concurrent ( cell = 1 : n ) & local ( k , e , nbr , cs2_area , inv_area , nu_max_cell , & h_k , u_c , v_c , u_nbr , v_nbr , u_edge , v_edge , & dudx , dvdy , dudy , dvdx , strain , nu_h , & dx_cn , dy_cn , d_cn , lap_u , lap_v ) if ( state % barotropic % height_cv ( cell ) < ML_MIN_DEPTH_UNSTR ) cycle inv_area = 1.0_wp / mesh % area ( cell ) cs2_area = cs * cs * mesh % area ( cell ) nu_max_cell = 0.125_wp * mesh % radius ( cell ) ** 2 / ( dt + 1.0e-30_wp ) do k = 1 , nz h_k = state % multilayer % h_layer ( k , cell ) if ( h_k < THIN_LAYER_THRESHOLD ) cycle u_c = hu_pre ( k , cell ) / h_k v_c = hv_pre ( k , cell ) / h_k ! ----- Velocity gradient via Green-Gauss ----- dudx = 0.0_wp dvdy = 0.0_wp dudy = 0.0_wp dvdx = 0.0_wp do e = 1 , 3 nbr = mesh % surrogate_neighbours ( e , cell ) if ( nbr == cell ) then u_edge = u_c v_edge = v_c else if ( state % multilayer % h_layer ( k , nbr ) < THIN_LAYER_THRESHOLD ) then u_edge = u_c v_edge = v_c else u_nbr = hu_pre ( k , nbr ) / state % multilayer % h_layer ( k , nbr ) v_nbr = hv_pre ( k , nbr ) / state % multilayer % h_layer ( k , nbr ) u_edge = 0.5_wp * ( u_c + u_nbr ) v_edge = 0.5_wp * ( v_c + v_nbr ) end if dudx = dudx + u_edge * mesh % edge_n1 ( e , cell ) * mesh % edge_len ( e , cell ) dudy = dudy + u_edge * mesh % edge_n2 ( e , cell ) * mesh % edge_len ( e , cell ) dvdx = dvdx + v_edge * mesh % edge_n1 ( e , cell ) * mesh % edge_len ( e , cell ) dvdy = dvdy + v_edge * mesh % edge_n2 ( e , cell ) * mesh % edge_len ( e , cell ) end do dudx = dudx * inv_area dudy = dudy * inv_area dvdx = dvdx * inv_area dvdy = dvdy * inv_area ! Strain rate magnitude strain = sqrt (( dudx - dvdy ) ** 2 + ( dudy + dvdx ) ** 2 ) ! Smagorinsky eddy viscosity with stability limiter nu_h = min ( cs2_area * strain , nu_max_cell ) ! ----- FVM Laplacian of velocity ----- lap_u = 0.0_wp lap_v = 0.0_wp do e = 1 , 3 nbr = mesh % surrogate_neighbours ( e , cell ) if ( nbr == cell ) cycle ! boundary — zero gradient if ( state % multilayer % h_layer ( k , nbr ) < THIN_LAYER_THRESHOLD ) cycle u_nbr = hu_pre ( k , nbr ) / state % multilayer % h_layer ( k , nbr ) v_nbr = hv_pre ( k , nbr ) / state % multilayer % h_layer ( k , nbr ) dx_cn = mesh % centroid_x ( nbr ) - mesh % centroid_x ( cell ) dy_cn = mesh % centroid_y ( nbr ) - mesh % centroid_y ( cell ) d_cn = sqrt ( dx_cn * dx_cn + dy_cn * dy_cn ) lap_u = lap_u + ( u_nbr - u_c ) * mesh % edge_len ( e , cell ) / d_cn lap_v = lap_v + ( v_nbr - v_c ) * mesh % edge_len ( e , cell ) / d_cn end do lap_u = lap_u * inv_area lap_v = lap_v * inv_area ! Update momentum state % multilayer % hu_layer ( k , cell ) = hu_pre ( k , cell ) + dt * h_k * nu_h * lap_u state % multilayer % hv_layer ( k , cell ) = hv_pre ( k , cell ) + dt * h_k * nu_h * lap_v end do end do end associate end subroutine ml_horizontal_viscosity_smag_unstr","tags":"","url":"proc/ml_horizontal_viscosity_smag_unstr.html"},{"title":"state_unstr_destroy – Rakali","text":"public  subroutine state_unstr_destroy(state) Release every allocatable array and reset counters. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( unstr_state_t ), intent(inout) :: state Calls proc~~state_unstr_destroy~~CallsGraph proc~state_unstr_destroy state_unstr_destroy proc~unstr_barotropic_destroy unstr_barotropic_state_t%unstr_barotropic_destroy proc~state_unstr_destroy->proc~unstr_barotropic_destroy Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Interface Interface Type Bound Procedure Type Bound Procedure Unknown Procedure Type Unknown Procedure Type Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a procedure to one which it calls. Dashed \narrows point from an interface to procedures which implement that interface.\nThis could include the module procedures in a generic interface or the\nimplementation in a submodule of an interface in a parent module. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Called by proc~~state_unstr_destroy~~CalledByGraph proc~state_unstr_destroy state_unstr_destroy proc~driver_run_coastal_unstr driver_run_coastal_unstr proc~driver_run_coastal_unstr->proc~state_unstr_destroy proc~state_unstr_init state_unstr_init proc~driver_run_coastal_unstr->proc~state_unstr_init proc~rki_unstr_destroy rki_unstr_destroy proc~rki_unstr_destroy->proc~state_unstr_destroy proc~state_unstr_init->proc~state_unstr_destroy proc~driver_run driver_run proc~driver_run->proc~driver_run_coastal_unstr proc~rki_unstr_create rki_unstr_create proc~rki_unstr_create->proc~state_unstr_init proc~rki_unstr_enable_multilayer rki_unstr_enable_multilayer proc~rki_unstr_enable_multilayer->proc~state_unstr_init Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Interface Interface Type Bound Procedure Type Bound Procedure Unknown Procedure Type Unknown Procedure Type Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a procedure to one which it calls. Dashed \narrows point from an interface to procedures which implement that interface.\nThis could include the module procedures in a generic interface or the\nimplementation in a submodule of an interface in a parent module. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Source Code subroutine state_unstr_destroy ( state ) !! Release every allocatable array and reset counters. type ( unstr_state_t ), intent ( inout ) :: state call state % barotropic % destroy () call state % multilayer % destroy () call state % forcing % destroy () call state % si % destroy () state % ncells_owned = 0 state % ncells_total = 0 state % dt = 0.0_wp state % time = 0.0_wp state % step_count = 0 state % mass_error = 0.0_wp state % use_multilayer = . false . state % use_semi_implicit = . false . state % dt_fixed = 0.0_wp end subroutine state_unstr_destroy","tags":"","url":"proc/state_unstr_destroy.html"},{"title":"state_unstr_enable_si – Rakali","text":"public  subroutine state_unstr_enable_si(state, mesh, precond, rtol, dt_fixed, ok, wetdry, n_sub, bsub_fill) Enable the semi-implicit (Casulli) free-surface mode on this state:\nrun the fail-loud TPFA mesh-quality gate, allocate the si slot\n(with the P3.2 multilayer column slots when use_multilayer , AND/OR\nthe P3.3 Casulli wet/dry slots when wetdry — the two slot groups\ngate independently, so 3D + wet/dry coexist), and stash the CG\ncontrols.  Call AFTER state_unstr_init (so the multilayer\nsub-state exists and nz_ml is known) and BEFORE state_unstr_enter_data . ok=.false. (gate failure or a bad\nprecond having already error-stopped) leaves use_semi_implicit off so the caller can abort cleanly. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( unstr_state_t ), intent(inout) :: state type( mesh_unstr_t ), intent(in) :: mesh integer, intent(in) :: precond SI_PRECOND_* (already parsed; MG = aggregation multigrid here). real(kind=wp), intent(in) :: rtol CG relative residual tolerance. real(kind=wp), intent(in) :: dt_fixed Fixed timestep (s); the SI mode bypasses the gravity-wave CFL. logical, intent(out) :: ok logical, intent(in), optional :: wetdry Casulli wetting/drying (2D barotropic + 3D sigma multilayer;\nthe linear b_sub fill is 2D-only — see si_bsub_ml_conflict).\nDefault .false. integer, intent(in), optional :: n_sub Subgrid sub-bottoms per cell (>= 1).  Default 1 (flat bottom). integer, intent(in), optional :: bsub_fill Sub-bottom fill mode ( SI_BSUB_FILL_* ).  Default 0 (FLAT). Calls proc~~state_unstr_enable_si~~CallsGraph proc~state_unstr_enable_si state_unstr_enable_si error error proc~state_unstr_enable_si->error proc~mesh_unstr_si_quality_gate mesh_unstr_si_quality_gate proc~state_unstr_enable_si->proc~mesh_unstr_si_quality_gate proc~si_bsub_ml_conflict si_bsub_ml_conflict proc~state_unstr_enable_si->proc~si_bsub_ml_conflict proc~si_unstr_state_build_mg semi_implicit_state_unstr_t%si_unstr_state_build_mg proc~state_unstr_enable_si->proc~si_unstr_state_build_mg proc~si_unstr_state_init semi_implicit_state_unstr_t%si_unstr_state_init proc~state_unstr_enable_si->proc~si_unstr_state_init proc~mesh_unstr_si_quality_gate->error to_char to_char proc~mesh_unstr_si_quality_gate->to_char proc~agglom_hierarchy_unstr_build agglom_hierarchy_unstr_build proc~si_unstr_state_build_mg->proc~agglom_hierarchy_unstr_build info info proc~agglom_hierarchy_unstr_build->info proc~agglom_level_destroy agglom_level_unstr_t%agglom_level_destroy proc~agglom_hierarchy_unstr_build->proc~agglom_level_destroy proc~aggregate_and_build_coarse aggregate_and_build_coarse proc~agglom_hierarchy_unstr_build->proc~aggregate_and_build_coarse proc~alloc_level_coeffs alloc_level_coeffs proc~agglom_hierarchy_unstr_build->proc~alloc_level_coeffs proc~build_level1_structure build_level1_structure proc~agglom_hierarchy_unstr_build->proc~build_level1_structure proc~galerkin_operator galerkin_operator proc~agglom_hierarchy_unstr_build->proc~galerkin_operator proc~itoa itoa proc~agglom_hierarchy_unstr_build->proc~itoa proc~level1_operator level1_operator proc~agglom_hierarchy_unstr_build->proc~level1_operator proc~trim_levels trim_levels proc~agglom_hierarchy_unstr_build->proc~trim_levels cursor cursor proc~aggregate_and_build_coarse->cursor proc~build_coarse_adjacency build_coarse_adjacency proc~aggregate_and_build_coarse->proc~build_coarse_adjacency proc~find_slot find_slot proc~galerkin_operator->proc~find_slot proc~move_level move_level proc~trim_levels->proc~move_level proc~push_unique push_unique proc~build_coarse_adjacency->proc~push_unique proc~find_slot->error proc~find_slot->proc~itoa Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Interface Interface Type Bound Procedure Type Bound Procedure Unknown Procedure Type Unknown Procedure Type Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a procedure to one which it calls. Dashed \narrows point from an interface to procedures which implement that interface.\nThis could include the module procedures in a generic interface or the\nimplementation in a submodule of an interface in a parent module. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Called by proc~~state_unstr_enable_si~~CalledByGraph proc~state_unstr_enable_si state_unstr_enable_si proc~driver_run_coastal_unstr driver_run_coastal_unstr proc~driver_run_coastal_unstr->proc~state_unstr_enable_si proc~driver_run driver_run proc~driver_run->proc~driver_run_coastal_unstr Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Interface Interface Type Bound Procedure Type Bound Procedure Unknown Procedure Type Unknown Procedure Type Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a procedure to one which it calls. Dashed \narrows point from an interface to procedures which implement that interface.\nThis could include the module procedures in a generic interface or the\nimplementation in a submodule of an interface in a parent module. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Variables Type Visibility Attributes Name Initial integer, private :: bf integer, private :: ns integer, private :: nzml logical, private :: wd Source Code subroutine state_unstr_enable_si ( state , mesh , precond , rtol , dt_fixed , ok , & wetdry , n_sub , bsub_fill ) !! Enable the semi-implicit (Casulli) free-surface mode on this state: !! run the fail-loud TPFA mesh-quality gate, allocate the `si` slot !! (with the P3.2 multilayer column slots when `use_multilayer`, AND/OR !! the P3.3 Casulli wet/dry slots when `wetdry` — the two slot groups !! gate independently, so 3D + wet/dry coexist), and stash the CG !! controls.  Call AFTER `state_unstr_init` (so the multilayer !! sub-state exists and `nz_ml` is known) and BEFORE !! `state_unstr_enter_data`.  `ok=.false.` (gate failure or a bad !! precond having already error-stopped) leaves `use_semi_implicit` !! off so the caller can abort cleanly. type ( unstr_state_t ), intent ( inout ) :: state type ( mesh_unstr_t ), intent ( in ) :: mesh integer , intent ( in ) :: precond !! `SI_PRECOND_*` (already parsed; MG = aggregation multigrid here). real ( wp ), intent ( in ) :: rtol !! CG relative residual tolerance. real ( wp ), intent ( in ) :: dt_fixed !! Fixed timestep (s); the SI mode bypasses the gravity-wave CFL. logical , intent ( out ) :: ok logical , intent ( in ), optional :: wetdry !! Casulli wetting/drying (2D barotropic + 3D sigma multilayer; !! the linear b_sub fill is 2D-only — see si_bsub_ml_conflict). !! Default .false. integer , intent ( in ), optional :: n_sub !! Subgrid sub-bottoms per cell (>= 1).  Default 1 (flat bottom). integer , intent ( in ), optional :: bsub_fill !! Sub-bottom fill mode (`SI_BSUB_FILL_*`).  Default 0 (FLAT). logical :: wd integer :: ns , nzml , bf wd = . false . if ( present ( wetdry )) wd = wetdry ns = 1 if ( present ( n_sub )) ns = max ( 1 , n_sub ) bf = 0 if ( present ( bsub_fill )) bf = bsub_fill ! CONSERVATION GUARD (fail-loud): the `linear` subgrid b_sub fill ! (n_sub >= 2) is INCOMPATIBLE with the multilayer (nz > 1) wet/dry ! step.  The free-surface eta-solve conserves the SUBGRID wetted volume ! v_cell(eta) = mean_s max(eta - b_sub_s, 0) (si_unstr_wetdry_volume), ! but `si_unstr_update_thickness_ml` and the recovery Dirichlet ghost ! consume max(eta - b, 0) on the PLAIN bed.  Under `flat` (b_sub == b) ! these are identical so mass conserves; under `linear` they diverge ! across the partial-flooding band => mass leaks at the wet/dry front — ! the exact defect the port exists to prevent.  A v_cell-consistent ML ! thickness update is a documented deferred limitation; until then ! `linear + ML` is rejected rather than run silently.  `flat + ML` ! (conservative, tested) and `linear + 2D` (barotropic uses v_cell ! throughout) both stay allowed. if ( si_bsub_ml_conflict ( bf , ns , state % use_multilayer , state % multilayer % nz_ml )) then call logger % error ( \"state_unstr_enable_si: wetdry_bsub_fill='linear' \" // & \"(n_sub>=2) is not supported with the multilayer \" // & \"(nz>1) wet/dry step — the ML thickness update \" // & \"consumes the plain bed, not the subgrid v_cell, so \" // & \"mass leaks at the wet/dry front.  Use \" // & \"wetdry_bsub_fill='flat' with multilayer, or 'linear' \" // & \"only with the 2D barotropic path.\" ) error stop \"state_unstr_enable_si: linear b_sub incompatible with multilayer wet/dry\" end if call mesh_unstr_si_quality_gate ( mesh , ok ) if (. not . ok ) return ! The multilayer column slots (nz>1) and the wet/dry slots gate ! INDEPENDENTLY in `si%init`, so 3D + wet/dry coexist: the ML wet/dry ! step (`si_unstr_step_ml`, wet/dry branch) reuses the mesh-agnostic ! V(eta)/a/Newton primitives + the dsig scale-together column drain. if ( state % use_multilayer . and . state % multilayer % nz_ml > 1 ) then nzml = state % multilayer % nz_ml call state % si % init ( mesh , nz = nzml , wetdry = wd , n_sub = ns ) else call state % si % init ( mesh , wetdry = wd , n_sub = ns ) end if state % si % bsub_fill = bf state % si % precond = precond state % si % rtol = rtol state % dt_fixed = dt_fixed state % use_semi_implicit = . true . ! Aggregation-MG preconditioner: v1 is the 2D barotropic path only ! (wet/dry Newton + multilayer column solves keep plain/Jacobi CG). ! Fail loud rather than silently ignore an MG request there. if ( precond == SI_PRECOND_MG ) then if ( wd . or . ( state % use_multilayer . and . state % multilayer % nz_ml > 1 )) then call logger % error ( \"state_unstr_enable_si: precond='mg' (aggregation \" // & \"multigrid) is 2D-barotropic only in v1 — the wet/dry \" // & \"Newton and multilayer column solves are not yet wired \" // & \"to the MG hierarchy.  Use precond='jacobi' with \" // & \"wet/dry or multilayer.\" ) error stop \"state_unstr_enable_si: mg precond not supported with wetdry/multilayer (v1)\" end if ! Build the (static) level hierarchy now (geometric aggregation; ! operator coefficients are filled per step by the refresh in the SI ! step).  Host-only; runs before enter_data. call state % si % build_mg ( mesh ) end if end subroutine state_unstr_enable_si","tags":"","url":"proc/state_unstr_enable_si.html"},{"title":"state_unstr_enter_data – Rakali","text":"public  subroutine state_unstr_enter_data(state) Map the unstructured state arrays into device memory. Called once after state_unstr_init and bathymetry/IC setup,\nbefore any kernel runs.  All subsequent kernel work happens\non the device until state_unstr_exit_data is called.\nMirrors rki_solver::solver_enter_data . Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( unstr_state_t ), intent(inout) :: state Calls proc~~state_unstr_enter_data~~CallsGraph proc~state_unstr_enter_data state_unstr_enter_data proc~tracers_enter_data tracers_enter_data proc~state_unstr_enter_data->proc~tracers_enter_data proc~unstr_barotropic_enter_data unstr_barotropic_state_t%unstr_barotropic_enter_data proc~state_unstr_enter_data->proc~unstr_barotropic_enter_data Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Interface Interface Type Bound Procedure Type Bound Procedure Unknown Procedure Type Unknown Procedure Type Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a procedure to one which it calls. Dashed \narrows point from an interface to procedures which implement that interface.\nThis could include the module procedures in a generic interface or the\nimplementation in a submodule of an interface in a parent module. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Called by proc~~state_unstr_enter_data~~CalledByGraph proc~state_unstr_enter_data state_unstr_enter_data proc~driver_run_coastal_unstr driver_run_coastal_unstr proc~driver_run_coastal_unstr->proc~state_unstr_enter_data proc~rki_unstr_finalize rki_unstr_finalize proc~rki_unstr_finalize->proc~state_unstr_enter_data proc~driver_run driver_run proc~driver_run->proc~driver_run_coastal_unstr Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Interface Interface Type Bound Procedure Type Bound Procedure Unknown Procedure Type Unknown Procedure Type Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a procedure to one which it calls. Dashed \narrows point from an interface to procedures which implement that interface.\nThis could include the module procedures in a generic interface or the\nimplementation in a submodule of an interface in a parent module. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Source Code subroutine state_unstr_enter_data ( state ) !! Map the unstructured state arrays into device memory. !! !! Called once after `state_unstr_init` and bathymetry/IC setup, !! before any kernel runs.  All subsequent kernel work happens !! on the device until `state_unstr_exit_data` is called. !! Mirrors `rki_solver::solver_enter_data`. type ( unstr_state_t ), intent ( inout ) :: state ! Map the parent struct descriptor first so the device knows the ! shape of `unstr_state_t`.  Components (and the value-type ! sub-objects' allocatables) attach to it below.  Without the ! parent map first, NVHPC stdpar can't dereference component ! arrays from inside a do-concurrent body. !$acc enter data copyin(state) call state % barotropic % enter_data () call state % forcing % enter_data () if ( state % use_semi_implicit ) then call state % si % enter_data () end if if ( state % use_multilayer ) then call state % multilayer % enter_data () ! Generic tracer registry: two-step attach pattern (parent ! array descriptor first, then per-element hTr/hTr0).  Mirrors ! the structured `solver_tracers_enter_data`.  Without this, ! NVHPC stdpar can't dereference `tracers(it)%hTr` from a ! do-concurrent body and the kernel hits CUDA_ERROR_ILLEGAL_ADDRESS. if ( allocated ( state % multilayer % tracers )) then call tracers_enter_data ( state ) end if end if end subroutine state_unstr_enter_data","tags":"","url":"proc/state_unstr_enter_data.html"},{"title":"state_unstr_exit_data – Rakali","text":"public  subroutine state_unstr_exit_data(state) Copy state arrays back from the device and release device\nmemory.  Counterpart to state_unstr_enter_data . Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( unstr_state_t ), intent(inout) :: state Calls proc~~state_unstr_exit_data~~CallsGraph proc~state_unstr_exit_data state_unstr_exit_data proc~tracers_exit_data tracers_exit_data proc~state_unstr_exit_data->proc~tracers_exit_data proc~unstr_multilayer_exit_data unstr_multilayer_state_t%unstr_multilayer_exit_data proc~state_unstr_exit_data->proc~unstr_multilayer_exit_data Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Interface Interface Type Bound Procedure Type Bound Procedure Unknown Procedure Type Unknown Procedure Type Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a procedure to one which it calls. Dashed \narrows point from an interface to procedures which implement that interface.\nThis could include the module procedures in a generic interface or the\nimplementation in a submodule of an interface in a parent module. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Called by proc~~state_unstr_exit_data~~CalledByGraph proc~state_unstr_exit_data state_unstr_exit_data proc~driver_run_coastal_unstr driver_run_coastal_unstr proc~driver_run_coastal_unstr->proc~state_unstr_exit_data proc~ensure_host ensure_host proc~ensure_host->proc~state_unstr_exit_data proc~driver_run driver_run proc~driver_run->proc~driver_run_coastal_unstr proc~rki_unstr_destroy rki_unstr_destroy proc~rki_unstr_destroy->proc~ensure_host Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Interface Interface Type Bound Procedure Type Bound Procedure Unknown Procedure Type Unknown Procedure Type Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a procedure to one which it calls. Dashed \narrows point from an interface to procedures which implement that interface.\nThis could include the module procedures in a generic interface or the\nimplementation in a submodule of an interface in a parent module. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Source Code subroutine state_unstr_exit_data ( state ) !! Copy state arrays back from the device and release device !! memory.  Counterpart to `state_unstr_enter_data`. type ( unstr_state_t ), intent ( inout ) :: state ! Components first, parent struct last (mirror of enter_data order). if ( state % use_multilayer ) then ! Generic tracer registry: per-element components first, then ! the array descriptor (mirror of enter_data order). if ( allocated ( state % multilayer % tracers )) then call tracers_exit_data ( state ) end if call state % multilayer % exit_data () end if if ( state % use_semi_implicit ) then call state % si % exit_data () end if call state % forcing % exit_data () call state % barotropic % exit_data () ! Parent struct descriptor last. !$acc exit data delete(state) end subroutine state_unstr_exit_data","tags":"","url":"proc/state_unstr_exit_data.html"},{"title":"state_unstr_init – Rakali","text":"public  subroutine state_unstr_init(state, mesh, nz_ml) Allocate every array in state to match the cell counts in mesh . All arrays are zero-initialised.  Calling on an\nalready-initialised state is safe – it destroys first. When nz_ml is present and >= 1, the multilayer sub-object\nis initialised: use_multilayer is set and state%multilayer allocates per-layer arrays + tracer registry.\nIf nz_ml is absent the state stays 2D depth-averaged. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( unstr_state_t ), intent(inout) :: state type( mesh_unstr_t ), intent(in) :: mesh integer, intent(in), optional :: nz_ml Calls proc~~state_unstr_init~~CallsGraph proc~state_unstr_init state_unstr_init error error proc~state_unstr_init->error info info proc~state_unstr_init->info proc~state_unstr_destroy state_unstr_destroy proc~state_unstr_init->proc~state_unstr_destroy proc~unstr_barotropic_init unstr_barotropic_state_t%unstr_barotropic_init proc~state_unstr_init->proc~unstr_barotropic_init to_char to_char proc~state_unstr_init->to_char proc~unstr_barotropic_destroy unstr_barotropic_state_t%unstr_barotropic_destroy proc~state_unstr_destroy->proc~unstr_barotropic_destroy proc~unstr_barotropic_init->proc~unstr_barotropic_destroy Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Interface Interface Type Bound Procedure Type Bound Procedure Unknown Procedure Type Unknown Procedure Type Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a procedure to one which it calls. Dashed \narrows point from an interface to procedures which implement that interface.\nThis could include the module procedures in a generic interface or the\nimplementation in a submodule of an interface in a parent module. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Called by proc~~state_unstr_init~~CalledByGraph proc~state_unstr_init state_unstr_init proc~driver_run_coastal_unstr driver_run_coastal_unstr proc~driver_run_coastal_unstr->proc~state_unstr_init proc~rki_unstr_create rki_unstr_create proc~rki_unstr_create->proc~state_unstr_init proc~rki_unstr_enable_multilayer rki_unstr_enable_multilayer proc~rki_unstr_enable_multilayer->proc~state_unstr_init proc~driver_run driver_run proc~driver_run->proc~driver_run_coastal_unstr Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Interface Interface Type Bound Procedure Type Bound Procedure Unknown Procedure Type Unknown Procedure Type Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a procedure to one which it calls. Dashed \narrows point from an interface to procedures which implement that interface.\nThis could include the module procedures in a generic interface or the\nimplementation in a submodule of an interface in a parent module. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Variables Type Visibility Attributes Name Initial integer, private :: n Source Code subroutine state_unstr_init ( state , mesh , nz_ml ) !! Allocate every array in `state` to match the cell counts in !! `mesh`. All arrays are zero-initialised.  Calling on an !! already-initialised state is safe -- it destroys first. !! !! When `nz_ml` is present and >= 1, the multilayer sub-object !! is initialised: `use_multilayer` is set and !! `state%multilayer` allocates per-layer arrays + tracer registry. !! If `nz_ml` is absent the state stays 2D depth-averaged. type ( unstr_state_t ), intent ( inout ) :: state type ( mesh_unstr_t ), intent ( in ) :: mesh integer , intent ( in ), optional :: nz_ml integer :: n call state_unstr_destroy ( state ) n = mesh % ncells_total state % ncells_owned = mesh % ncells_owned state % ncells_total = n call state % barotropic % init ( mesh ) call state % forcing % init ( mesh ) state % dt = 0.0_wp state % time = 0.0_wp state % step_count = 0 state % mass_error = 0.0_wp if ( present ( nz_ml )) then if ( nz_ml < 1 ) then call logger % error ( \"state_unstr: nz_ml must be >= 1 (got \" // & to_char ( nz_ml ) // \")\" ) return end if state % use_multilayer = . true . call state % multilayer % init ( mesh , nz_ml ) call logger % info ( \"state_unstr: multilayer enabled with nz_ml = \" // & to_char ( nz_ml )) end if call logger % info ( \"state_unstr: allocated for \" // to_char ( state % ncells_owned ) // & \" owned cells (\" // to_char ( n ) // \" total)\" ) end subroutine state_unstr_init","tags":"","url":"proc/state_unstr_init.html"},{"title":"tracers_enter_data – Rakali","text":"private  subroutine tracers_enter_data(state) Per-element copyin for the tracer registry.  Split out so the\nenclosing routine stays linear. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( unstr_state_t ), intent(inout) :: state Called by proc~~tracers_enter_data~~CalledByGraph proc~tracers_enter_data tracers_enter_data proc~state_unstr_enter_data state_unstr_enter_data proc~state_unstr_enter_data->proc~tracers_enter_data proc~driver_run_coastal_unstr driver_run_coastal_unstr proc~driver_run_coastal_unstr->proc~state_unstr_enter_data proc~rki_unstr_finalize rki_unstr_finalize proc~rki_unstr_finalize->proc~state_unstr_enter_data proc~driver_run driver_run proc~driver_run->proc~driver_run_coastal_unstr Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Interface Interface Type Bound Procedure Type Bound Procedure Unknown Procedure Type Unknown Procedure Type Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a procedure to one which it calls. Dashed \narrows point from an interface to procedures which implement that interface.\nThis could include the module procedures in a generic interface or the\nimplementation in a submodule of an interface in a parent module. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Variables Type Visibility Attributes Name Initial integer, private :: it Source Code subroutine tracers_enter_data ( state ) !! Per-element copyin for the tracer registry.  Split out so the !! enclosing routine stays linear. type ( unstr_state_t ), intent ( inout ) :: state integer :: it !$acc enter data copyin(state%multilayer%tracers) do it = 1 , size ( state % multilayer % tracers ) !$acc enter data copyin(state%multilayer%tracers(it)%hTr, & !$acc&                  state%multilayer%tracers(it)%hTr0) end do ! The device map above is an element-by-element snapshot: growing ! the registry after this point would strand every mapping. ! register_passive_tracer refuses while locked. state % multilayer % registry_locked = . true . end subroutine tracers_enter_data","tags":"","url":"proc/tracers_enter_data.html"},{"title":"tracers_exit_data – Rakali","text":"private  subroutine tracers_exit_data(state) Per-element copyout/delete for the tracer registry. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( unstr_state_t ), intent(inout) :: state Called by proc~~tracers_exit_data~~CalledByGraph proc~tracers_exit_data tracers_exit_data proc~state_unstr_exit_data state_unstr_exit_data proc~state_unstr_exit_data->proc~tracers_exit_data proc~driver_run_coastal_unstr driver_run_coastal_unstr proc~driver_run_coastal_unstr->proc~state_unstr_exit_data proc~ensure_host ensure_host proc~ensure_host->proc~state_unstr_exit_data proc~driver_run driver_run proc~driver_run->proc~driver_run_coastal_unstr proc~rki_unstr_destroy rki_unstr_destroy proc~rki_unstr_destroy->proc~ensure_host Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Interface Interface Type Bound Procedure Type Bound Procedure Unknown Procedure Type Unknown Procedure Type Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a procedure to one which it calls. Dashed \narrows point from an interface to procedures which implement that interface.\nThis could include the module procedures in a generic interface or the\nimplementation in a submodule of an interface in a parent module. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Variables Type Visibility Attributes Name Initial integer, private :: it Source Code subroutine tracers_exit_data ( state ) !! Per-element copyout/delete for the tracer registry. type ( unstr_state_t ), intent ( inout ) :: state integer :: it do it = 1 , size ( state % multilayer % tracers ) !$acc exit data copyout(state%multilayer%tracers(it)%hTr) !$acc exit data delete(state%multilayer%tracers(it)%hTr0) end do !$acc exit data delete(state%multilayer%tracers) state % multilayer % registry_locked = . false . end subroutine tracers_exit_data","tags":"","url":"proc/tracers_exit_data.html"},{"title":"compute_hfactor_impl – Rakali","text":"private pure function compute_hfactor_impl(hc, h0, h1, h2, min_h) result(hfactor) Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name real(kind=wp), intent(in) :: hc real(kind=wp), intent(in) :: h0 real(kind=wp), intent(in) :: h1 real(kind=wp), intent(in) :: h2 real(kind=wp), intent(in) :: min_h Return Value real(kind=wp) Called by proc~~compute_hfactor_impl~~CalledByGraph proc~compute_hfactor_impl compute_hfactor_impl proc~extrapolate_three_neighbour_branch extrapolate_three_neighbour_branch proc~extrapolate_three_neighbour_branch->proc~compute_hfactor_impl proc~extrapolate_one_cell extrapolate_one_cell proc~extrapolate_one_cell->proc~extrapolate_three_neighbour_branch proc~per_cell_extrapolation per_cell_extrapolation proc~per_cell_extrapolation->proc~extrapolate_one_cell proc~extrapolate_second_order_edge_unstr extrapolate_second_order_edge_unstr proc~extrapolate_second_order_edge_unstr->proc~per_cell_extrapolation proc~solver_unstr_advance solver_unstr_advance proc~solver_unstr_advance->proc~extrapolate_second_order_edge_unstr proc~solver_unstr_step solver_unstr_step proc~solver_unstr_step->proc~extrapolate_second_order_edge_unstr proc~solver_unstr_step_generic solver_unstr_step_generic proc~solver_unstr_step_generic->proc~extrapolate_second_order_edge_unstr Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Interface Interface Type Bound Procedure Type Bound Procedure Unknown Procedure Type Unknown Procedure Type Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a procedure to one which it calls. Dashed \narrows point from an interface to procedures which implement that interface.\nThis could include the module procedures in a generic interface or the\nimplementation in a submodule of an interface in a parent module. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Variables Type Visibility Attributes Name Initial real(kind=wp), private :: hmax real(kind=wp), private :: hmin real(kind=wp), private :: tmp1 real(kind=wp), private :: tmp2 Source Code pure function compute_hfactor_impl ( hc , h0 , h1 , h2 , min_h ) result ( hfactor ) !$acc routine seq real ( wp ), intent ( in ) :: hc , h0 , h1 , h2 , min_h real ( wp ) :: hfactor real ( wp ) :: hmin , hmax , tmp1 , tmp2 hmin = min ( hc , h0 , h1 , h2 ) hmax = max ( hc , h0 , h1 , h2 ) tmp1 = HFACTOR_C * max ( hmin , 0.0_wp ) / max ( hc , HFACTOR_HC_FLOOR ) + HFACTOR_D tmp2 = HFACTOR_C * max ( hc , 0.0_wp ) / max ( hmax , HFACTOR_HC_FLOOR ) + HFACTOR_D hfactor = max ( 0.0_wp , min ( tmp1 , tmp2 , 1.0_wp )) hfactor = min ( 1.2_wp * max ( hmin - min_h , 0.0_wp ) & / ( max ( hmin , 0.0_wp ) + min_h ), hfactor ) end function compute_hfactor_impl","tags":"","url":"proc/compute_hfactor_impl.html"},{"title":"extrapolate_second_order_edge_unstr – Rakali","text":"public  subroutine extrapolate_second_order_edge_unstr(state, mesh) Reconstruct edge-midpoint values from centroid values. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( unstr_state_t ), intent(inout) :: state type( mesh_unstr_t ), intent(in) :: mesh Calls proc~~extrapolate_second_order_edge_unstr~~CallsGraph proc~extrapolate_second_order_edge_unstr extrapolate_second_order_edge_unstr proc~centroid_pass centroid_pass proc~extrapolate_second_order_edge_unstr->proc~centroid_pass proc~centroid_restore centroid_restore proc~extrapolate_second_order_edge_unstr->proc~centroid_restore proc~per_cell_extrapolation per_cell_extrapolation proc~extrapolate_second_order_edge_unstr->proc~per_cell_extrapolation proc~extrapolate_one_cell extrapolate_one_cell proc~per_cell_extrapolation->proc~extrapolate_one_cell proc~extrapolate_one_neighbour_branch extrapolate_one_neighbour_branch proc~extrapolate_one_cell->proc~extrapolate_one_neighbour_branch proc~extrapolate_three_neighbour_branch ex