 Subroutine Function Function Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a submodule to the (sub)module which it is\ndescended from. Dashed arrows point from a module or program unit to \nmodules which it uses. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Used by module~~rki_init~~UsedByGraph module~rki_init rki_init module~rki_api rki_api module~rki_api->module~rki_init module~rki_coastal_console_stats rki_coastal_console_stats module~rki_coastal_console_stats->module~rki_init module~rki_driver rki_driver module~rki_driver->module~rki_init module~rki_driver->module~rki_coastal_console_stats Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Module Module Submodule Submodule Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a submodule to the (sub)module which it is\ndescended from. Dashed arrows point from a module or program unit to \nmodules which it uses. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Functions public  function compute_total_mass (s) result(mass) Sum h * dx * dy over interior cells via OpenACC reduction (only\nthe scalar crosses the PCIe bus). Runs on host or device; NaN /\ndry-cell garbage is filtered. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( state_t ), intent(inout) :: s Return Value real(kind=wp) Subroutines public  subroutine compute_layer_sums (s, sum_h, sum_hS, sum_hT) Sum h_layer, hS_layer, hT_layer over interior wet cells and layers,\nreducing on device (three scalars cross the PCIe bus). Caller forms\nsum_hS/sum_h and sum_hT/sum_h for volume-weighted mean S and T. Read more… Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( state_t ), intent(inout) :: s real(kind=wp), intent(out) :: sum_h real(kind=wp), intent(out) :: sum_hS real(kind=wp), intent(out) :: sum_hT public  subroutine diagnose_bathymetry (s, cfg) Report bathymetry diagnostics and warn about potential CFL issues:\nextreme depths, steep wet-to-wet gradients, shallow-next-to-deep\ncells (velocity blowup risk), and the estimated CFL timestep. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( state_t ), intent(in) :: s type( config_t ), intent(in) :: cfg public  subroutine init_castro_dam_break (s) Initialise the circular dam break from Castro et al. (2011):\ndomain [-5,5]&#94;2, Gaussian bump bathymetry, circular water column\n(r <= 0.6: eta = 3, else eta = 1). Public only for the unit-test suite. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( state_t ), intent(inout) :: s public  subroutine init_dam_break (s) Initialise a 1D dam break (left half h=2m, right half h=1m, no\nvelocity). Bathymetry preserved if already loaded, else zeroed.\nPublic only for the unit-test suite. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( state_t ), intent(inout) :: s public  subroutine init_from_config (s, cfg) Select and apply initial condition based on config Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( state_t ), intent(inout) :: s type( config_t ), intent(in) :: cfg public  subroutine init_gaussian_hump (s, h0, amplitude, sigma, x_off, y_off, u_shear) Initialise a Gaussian hump perturbation. When u_shear /= 0 and NH\nis enabled, the top layer gets +u_shear and the bottom -u_shear\n(vertical shear → non-zero vorticity). Bathymetry preserved if\nalready loaded. Public only for the unit-test suite. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( state_t ), intent(inout) :: s real(kind=wp), intent(in) :: h0 Background water depth (m) real(kind=wp), intent(in) :: amplitude Hump amplitude (m) real(kind=wp), intent(in) :: sigma Gaussian width (m) real(kind=wp), intent(in) :: x_off Hump centre x-offset from domain centre (m) real(kind=wp), intent(in) :: y_off Hump centre y-offset from domain centre (m) real(kind=wp), intent(in) :: u_shear Initial vertical shear: top +val, bottom -val (m/s) public  subroutine init_multilayer_from_barotropic (s, cfg) Cold-start multilayer initialisation from the barotropic state:\nlay out the layers, seed the tracers from their IC scalars, then\nderive density and the reference-density profile. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( state_t ), intent(inout) :: s type( config_t ), intent(in) :: cfg public  subroutine init_still_water (s, eta0) Initialise a flat free surface at elevation eta0:\nh = max(eta0 - b, 0) everywhere, zero velocity. Requires\nbathymetry already loaded into s%barotropic%b. Public only for tests. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( state_t ), intent(inout) :: s real(kind=wp), intent(in) :: eta0 Initial free-surface elevation (m, same datum as bathymetry) public  subroutine refresh_multilayer_derived (s) Recompute the multilayer fields that are derived from the layer\nthicknesses and tracers: the equation of state ( rho_layer ) and\nthe reference-density profile ( rho_ref ) that the baroclinic\npressure-gradient kernels subtract. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( state_t ), intent(inout) :: s public  subroutine restore_multilayer_after_restart (s) A restart file restores the prognostic multilayer fields (layer\nthicknesses, momenta, tracers) but not the derived ones.  Rebuild\nthe z*-full reference levels and the density / reference-density\nprofile so a warm start sees the same state a continuous run does. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( state_t ), intent(inout) :: s private  subroutine compute_layer_sums_impl (h_layer, hS_layer, hT_layer, grid, nz, sum_h, sum_hS, sum_hT) Inner reduction kernel for compute_layer_sums; takes flat 3D arrays\nso the device kernel avoids the tracer-registry indirection. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name real(kind=wp), intent(in) :: h_layer (grid%nx_total,grid%ny_total,nz) real(kind=wp), intent(in) :: hS_layer (grid%nx_total,grid%ny_total,nz) real(kind=wp), intent(in) :: hT_layer (grid%nx_total,grid%ny_total,nz) type( hgrid_t ), intent(in) :: grid integer, intent(in) :: nz real(kind=wp), intent(out) :: sum_h real(kind=wp), intent(out) :: sum_hS real(kind=wp), intent(out) :: sum_hT","tags":"","url":"module/rki_init.html"},{"title":"rki_remap_column – Rakali","text":"Pure per-column conservative vertical remap, shared by both backends.\nReconstruction methods:\n  PCM    — piecewise constant (donor cell)\n  PLM    — piecewise linear, minmod limiter\n  PPM    — piecewise parabolic (Colella & Woodward 1984)\n  PPM_H4 — PPM with non-uniform 4th-order edge values (White & Adcroft 2008)\n  PQM    — piecewise quartic (White & Adcroft 2008) All routines are pure with NZ_STACK_MAX stack workspace so they run\ninside do concurrent (one GPU thread per column, sequential O(nz)\nvertical sweep). Conservation: sum(q_new dz_new) = sum(q_old dz_old)\nto machine precision when sum(dz_old) = sum(dz_new). Uses rki_constants module~~rki_remap_column~~UsesGraph module~rki_remap_column rki_remap_column module~rki_constants rki_constants module~rki_remap_column->module~rki_constants pic_types pic_types module~rki_constants->pic_types Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Module Module Submodule Submodule Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a submodule to the (sub)module which it is\ndescended from. Dashed arrows point from a module or program unit to \nmodules which it uses. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Used by module~~rki_remap_column~~UsedByGraph module~rki_remap_column rki_remap_column module~rki_ml_dynamics rki_ml_dynamics module~rki_ml_dynamics->module~rki_remap_column module~rki_ml_dynamics_unstr rki_ml_dynamics_unstr module~rki_ml_dynamics_unstr->module~rki_remap_column module~rki_ml_tracers rki_ml_tracers module~rki_ml_tracers->module~rki_remap_column module~rki_multilayer_state rki_multilayer_state module~rki_multilayer_state->module~rki_ml_dynamics module~rki_multilayer_state->module~rki_ml_tracers module~rki_si_unstr_step_ml rki_si_unstr_step_ml module~rki_si_unstr_step_ml->module~rki_ml_dynamics_unstr module~rki_solver rki_solver module~rki_solver->module~rki_ml_dynamics module~rki_solver->module~rki_ml_tracers module~rki_state rki_state module~rki_solver->module~rki_state module~rki_solver_unstr rki_solver_unstr module~rki_solver_unstr->module~rki_ml_dynamics_unstr module~rki_solver_unstr->module~rki_si_unstr_step_ml module~rki_api rki_api module~rki_api->module~rki_solver module~rki_api->module~rki_state module~rki_handle rki_handle module~rki_api->module~rki_handle module~rki_init rki_init module~rki_api->module~rki_init module~rki_output rki_output module~rki_api->module~rki_output module~rki_api_unstr rki_api_unstr module~rki_api_unstr->module~rki_solver_unstr module~rki_api_unstr->module~rki_handle module~rki_driver rki_driver module~rki_driver->module~rki_solver module~rki_driver->module~rki_solver_unstr module~rki_driver->module~rki_state module~rki_coastal_console_stats rki_coastal_console_stats module~rki_driver->module~rki_coastal_console_stats module~rki_forcing rki_forcing module~rki_driver->module~rki_forcing module~rki_gauges rki_gauges module~rki_driver->module~rki_gauges module~rki_driver->module~rki_init module~rki_nesting rki_nesting module~rki_driver->module~rki_nesting module~rki_driver->module~rki_output module~rki_state->module~rki_multilayer_state module~rki_coastal_console_stats->module~rki_state module~rki_coastal_console_stats->module~rki_init module~rki_flux_dump rki_flux_dump module~rki_flux_dump->module~rki_state module~rki_forcing->module~rki_state module~rki_gauges->module~rki_state module~rki_handle->module~rki_state module~rki_init->module~rki_state module~rki_nesting->module~rki_state module~rki_output->module~rki_state Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Module Module Submodule Submodule Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a submodule to the (sub)module which it is\ndescended from. Dashed arrows point from a module or program unit to \nmodules which it uses. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Variables Type Visibility Attributes Name Initial real(kind=wp), private, parameter :: H_MIN_FRAC = 1.0e-5_wp Relative thickness floor in the PPM_H4 edge stencil when a consecutive\nthickness pair sums to ~0. real(kind=wp), private, parameter :: H_NEGLECT = 1.0e-30_wp Tiny thickness floor for the PPM_H4 edge singularity guard; only fires\non degenerate (vanishing-layer) columns. White & Adcroft 2008. real(kind=wp), private, parameter :: H_RATIO_FLOOR = 1.0e-12_wp Relative thickness-ratio floor in the PQM implicit-h4 edge-value\nstencil (White & Adcroft 2008, roundoff-safe). real(kind=wp), private, parameter :: PQM_MIN_FRAC = 1.0e-6_wp Boundary-closure guard for the PQM 4th-order one-sided end fit\n(White & Adcroft 2008). Subroutines public pure subroutine remap_column (method, nz, dz_old, dz_new, q_old, q_new) Dispatch to the requested remapping method. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name integer, intent(in) :: method REMAP_PCM, REMAP_PLM, REMAP_PPM, REMAP_PPM_H4, or REMAP_PQM integer, intent(in) :: nz real(kind=wp), intent(in) :: dz_old (nz) real(kind=wp), intent(in) :: dz_new (nz) real(kind=wp), intent(in) :: q_old (nz) real(kind=wp), intent(out) :: q_new (nz) public pure subroutine remap_column_pcm (nz, dz_old, dz_new, q_old, q_new) Piecewise-constant (donor cell) remap. Diffusive, guaranteed monotone. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name integer, intent(in) :: nz real(kind=wp), intent(in) :: dz_old (nz) Old layer thicknesses (must sum to same total as dz_new) real(kind=wp), intent(in) :: dz_new (nz) New layer thicknesses real(kind=wp), intent(in) :: q_old (nz) Old cell-average scalar values real(kind=wp), intent(out) :: q_new (nz) New cell-average scalar values (conservative) public pure subroutine remap_column_plm (nz, dz_old, dz_new, q_old, q_new) Piecewise-linear (minmod-limited) remap. Monotone (no new extrema).\nPer old layer k: q_hat(xi) = q(k) + slope(k) (2 xi - 1), xi in [0,1],\nslope(k) = 0.5*minmod(q(k+1)-q(k), q(k)-q(k-1)). Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name integer, intent(in) :: nz real(kind=wp), intent(in) :: dz_old (nz) Old layer thicknesses real(kind=wp), intent(in) :: dz_new (nz) New layer thicknesses real(kind=wp), intent(in) :: q_old (nz) Old cell-average scalar values real(kind=wp), intent(out) :: q_new (nz) New cell-average scalar values (conservative) public pure subroutine remap_column_ppm (nz, dz_old, dz_new, q_old, q_new) Piecewise-parabolic (Colella & Woodward 1984) remap.\nPer old layer k, xi in [0,1]:\n  q_hat(xi) = q_L + xi (q_R - q_L + q6 (1 - xi)), q6 = 6 q_bar - 3 (q_L+q_R)\nEdge values: 4th-order interp + CW monotonicity limiting; boundary\nlayers fall back to PLM-quality edges. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name integer, intent(in) :: nz real(kind=wp), intent(in) :: dz_old (nz) Old layer thicknesses real(kind=wp), intent(in) :: dz_new (nz) New layer thicknesses real(kind=wp), intent(in) :: q_old (nz) Old cell-average scalar values real(kind=wp), intent(out) :: q_new (nz) New cell-average scalar values (conservative) public pure subroutine remap_column_ppm_h4 (nz, dz_old, dz_new, q_old, q_new) PPM with non-uniform 4th-order (H4) edge values (White & Adcroft 2008).\nAs remap_column_ppm but the interior edge estimate is the\nthickness-weighted exactly-4th-order stencil (reduces to PPM’s\n(7/12,-1/12) on uniform layers), cutting spurious diapycnal mixing per\nremap (Ilicak et al. 2012). Limiter/reconstruction/integration are\nidentical to PPM. H4 edge algebra is inlined (helper extraction costs\n4-6% on this hot kernel). Boundary edges: outermost = PCM,\nsecond-from-boundary = non-uniform 3-cell (H3) quadratic; CW limiter\nclamps all edges to local monotone bounds. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name integer, intent(in) :: nz real(kind=wp), intent(in) :: dz_old (nz) Old layer thicknesses real(kind=wp), intent(in) :: dz_new (nz) New layer thicknesses real(kind=wp), intent(in) :: q_old (nz) Old cell-average scalar values real(kind=wp), intent(out) :: q_new (nz) New cell-average scalar values (conservative) public pure subroutine remap_column_pqm (nz, dz_old, dz_new, q_old, q_new) Piecewise-quartic (PQM_IH4IH3) conservative remap (White & Adcroft 2008).\nImplicit-h4 edge VALUES + implicit-h3 edge SLOPES (each a\ndiagonally-dominant tridiagonal solve with one-sided 4-cell boundary\nclosure), per-cell quartic, W&A monotonicity limiter, conservative\nquartic overlap integral. Cuts diapycnal mixing per remap vs PPM/PPM_H4\n(Ilicak et al. 2012).\nPer cell k, xi in [0,1]: q_hat(xi) = a + b xi + c xi&#94;2 + d xi&#94;3 + e xi&#94;4.\nBoundary cells reconstruct as PCM. nz < 5 falls back to REMAP_PPM\n(W&A boundary closure needs >= 4 cells). Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name integer, intent(in) :: nz real(kind=wp), intent(in) :: dz_old (nz) Old layer thicknesses (must sum to same total as dz_new) real(kind=wp), intent(in) :: dz_new (nz) New layer thicknesses real(kind=wp), intent(in) :: q_old (nz) Old cell-average scalar values real(kind=wp), intent(out) :: q_new (nz) New cell-average scalar values (conservative) private pure subroutine pqm_end_value_h4 (dz, u, csys) One-sided 4th-order polynomial fit of the cell averages u to the\nfour boundary layers dz (thicknesses, must be positive), returning\nthe four coefficients csys of the fit (White & Adcroft 2008,\nappendix; roundoff-safe closed form). csys(1) is the edge VALUE at\nthe boundary interface and csys(2) is the edge SLOPE there. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name real(kind=wp), intent(in) :: dz (4) Thicknesses of the 4 boundary layers, starting at the edge real(kind=wp), intent(in) :: u (4) Cell averages of the 4 boundary layers, starting at the edge real(kind=wp), intent(out) :: csys (4) Coefficients of the 4th-order fit polynomial in z private pure subroutine pqm_solve_diag_dominant (n, al, ac, au, r, x) Diagonally-dominant tridiagonal solve; central diagonal supplied as the\nOFFSET ac from al + au (full pivot = ac + al + au). Never divides by\nzero for positive-definite ac, al, au (White & Adcroft 2008). Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name integer, intent(in) :: n Number of unknowns (= number of edges = nz+1) real(kind=wp), intent(in) :: al (n) Lower diagonal (al(1) unused) real(kind=wp), intent(in) :: ac (n) Central-diagonal OFFSET from al+au (full diagonal = ac+al+au) real(kind=wp), intent(in) :: au (n) Upper diagonal (au(n) unused) real(kind=wp), intent(in) :: r (n) Right-hand side real(kind=wp), intent(out) :: x (n) Solution vector","tags":"","url":"module/rki_remap_column.html"},{"title":"rki_boundary_unstr – Rakali","text":"Integer constants identifying the boundary condition type for\neach boundary edge group, plus a fixed maximum on the number of\ngroups a single mesh can carry. Mirrors the role of rki_boundary_types on the structured side. Seven BC types are\nfully dispatched inside compute_boundary_state_impl (inlined into rki_kernel_flux_unstr ): BC_WALL , BC_OPEN , BC_INFLOW , BC_DISCHARGE , BC_OUTFLOW , BC_TIDAL , BC_CLAMPED . BC_CHAPMAN is dispatched one level higher in the flux kernel\n( rki_kernel_flux_unstr ) because it needs per-edge mesh access to\ncompute the radiating ghost target before falling through into the\nFlather/ BC_TIDAL algebra. The multilayer tracer advection kernel\n( ml_advect_tracer_unstr ) honours bc_S_ext / bc_T_ext at\ninflow edges, so freshwater / clamped-tracer river inflows work\nend-to-end. Types that live on the structured side but are not yet\nported: BC_NESTED , BC_SPONGE . BC_TIDAL is a Flather radiating boundary that clamps the ghost\nstage to bc_eta_ext(g) and reconstructs the ghost outward-normal\nvelocity from the outgoing Riemann invariant. BC_CLAMPED does\nthe same stage clamp but copies momentum transmissively (no\nradiation term) – pick it when you want a pure Dirichlet stage\nwithout the wave-radiation coupling. How a mesh wires up its boundary edges: Every boundary edge of a triangle has mesh%neighbours(i, k) == -1 and mesh%bc_group(i, k) >= 0 . Group 0 means “no group set\n    – treat as default wall”. state%bc_type(g) for g = 1..MAX_BC_GROUPS says what BC\n    type group g represents. Defaults to BC_WALL so a fresh\n    state with no explicit BC setup behaves like an all-walls\n    domain (which is what we want for lake-at-rest tests). The flux kernel reads the bc_type via this lookup and\n    dispatches inside compute_boundary_state_impl (inlined in rki_kernel_flux_unstr ) to build the right-side ghost state. Tracer inflow values live in state%bc_S_ext(g) / state%bc_T_ext(g) and are consumed by ml_advect_tracer_unstr on edges whose mass flux is into\n    the domain. Integer values match the structured side\n( rki_boundary_types ): WALL=1, OPEN=2, TIDAL=3, NESTED=4,\nINFLOW=5, DISCHARGE=6, CLAMPED=7, SPONGE=8, CHAPMAN=9. Only the\ntypes actually dispatched here are re-declared; the rest of that\nrange is reserved. BC_OUTFLOW = 10 is unstructured-only and\nsits past the structured range to avoid future collisions. Used by module~~rki_boundary_unstr~~UsedByGraph module~rki_boundary_unstr rki_boundary_unstr module~rki_api_unstr rki_api_unstr module~rki_api_unstr->module~rki_boundary_unstr module~rki_state_unstr rki_state_unstr module~rki_api_unstr->module~rki_state_unstr module~rki_gauges_unstr rki_gauges_unstr module~rki_api_unstr->module~rki_gauges_unstr module~rki_handle rki_handle module~rki_api_unstr->module~rki_handle module~rki_kernel_timestep_unstr rki_kernel_timestep_unstr module~rki_api_unstr->module~rki_kern