uish in\nlarge graphs. Subroutines public pure subroutine apply_sponge (h, hu, hv, grid, dt, bc) Apply sponge layer relaxation to h, hu, hv Read more… Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name real(kind=wp), intent(inout) :: h (grid%nx_total,grid%ny_total) real(kind=wp), intent(inout) :: hu (grid%nx_total,grid%ny_total) real(kind=wp), intent(inout) :: hv (grid%nx_total,grid%ny_total) type( hgrid_t ), intent(in) :: grid real(kind=wp), intent(in) :: dt Timestep (s) type( bc_params_t ), intent(in) :: bc Boundary condition parameters (sponge config)","tags":"","url":"module/rki_kernel_sponge.html"},{"title":"rki_si_unstr_state – Rakali","text":"Persistent workspace for the semi-implicit (Casulli) free-surface\nmode on the UNSTRUCTURED (triangular) backend\n( time_scheme=\"semi_implicit\" , grid_type=\"unstructured\" ).  The\nsemi-implicit workspace for the unstructured backend; allocated only when the mode is\non and living at state%si on unstr_state_t . GEOMETRY OF RECORD (cell-centred FV on a triangulation, prototype si_unstr_proto.py ): eta lives at the triangle CIRCUMCENTRE (mesh circumcentre_x/y , NOT the centroid the KNP path uses); the SI face\ntransports live on EDGES.  A triangle’s three edges are stored in the (3, ncell) per-edge-of-cell layout (edge i opposite vertex i) — the\nsame double-visit layout as the KNP flux path: each cell owns its 3\nedge values, interior edges are computed twice (no scatter, no\natomics, GPU-race-free). THE LOAD-BEARING CG DETAIL: the cell-centred FV Helmholtz operator is\nself-adjoint in the AREA-WEIGHTED inner product <x,y> = sum_c area_c\nx_c y_c , NOT the plain dot (variable cell areas scale the rows).  The\nCG/Jacobi dot products carry the mesh%area weight; the residual gate\nuses the area-weighted norm.  Get it wrong and the operator is\nnon-symmetric and CG stalls.  Uniform area recovers the structured\nharness.  (See rki_si_unstr_helmholtz .) All CG vectors are (ncell) ; the per-edge workspaces are (3, ncell) .\nThe enter_data / exit_data methods attach the arrays to the device;\nthe parent unstr_state_t struct is mapped by state_unstr_enter_data BEFORE this runs (same discipline as the structured si slot). Uses rki_mesh_unstr rki_constants pic_logger rki_si_unstr_mg module~~rki_si_unstr_state~~UsesGraph module~rki_si_unstr_state rki_si_unstr_state module~rki_constants rki_constants module~rki_si_unstr_state->module~rki_constants module~rki_mesh_unstr rki_mesh_unstr module~rki_si_unstr_state->module~rki_mesh_unstr module~rki_si_unstr_mg rki_si_unstr_mg module~rki_si_unstr_state->module~rki_si_unstr_mg pic_logger pic_logger module~rki_si_unstr_state->pic_logger pic_types pic_types module~rki_constants->pic_types module~rki_mesh_unstr->module~rki_constants module~rki_mesh_unstr->pic_logger pic_io pic_io module~rki_mesh_unstr->pic_io module~rki_mesh_unstr->pic_types module~rki_si_unstr_mg->module~rki_constants module~rki_si_unstr_mg->module~rki_mesh_unstr module~rki_si_unstr_mg->pic_logger Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Module Module Submodule Submodule Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a submodule to the (sub)module which it is\ndescended from. Dashed arrows point from a module or program unit to \nmodules which it uses. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Used by module~~rki_si_unstr_state~~UsedByGraph module~rki_si_unstr_state rki_si_unstr_state module~rki_driver rki_driver module~rki_driver->module~rki_si_unstr_state module~rki_state_unstr rki_state_unstr module~rki_driver->module~rki_state_unstr module~rki_solver_unstr rki_solver_unstr module~rki_driver->module~rki_solver_unstr module~rki_si_unstr_step rki_si_unstr_step module~rki_si_unstr_step->module~rki_si_unstr_state module~rki_si_unstr_step->module~rki_state_unstr module~rki_state_unstr->module~rki_si_unstr_sta